window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-cell-cycle","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division · Practice","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In sexually reproducing organisms, the chromosome number of a species stays constant from one generation to the next even though meiosis halves the chromosome number.\nReason (R): Fertilisation brings together two haploid gametes and restores the diploid phase.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the chromosome number of a species stays constant across generations, even though meiosis halves it.\nR is true: fertilisation fuses two haploid gametes and restores the diploid phase.\nR explains A: meiosis halving the number and fertilisation restoring it together conserve the chromosome number.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"During which process do we come across meiosis in both plants and animals?","image":"","options":["Growth of root and shoot apices","Repair of the gut lining","Gametogenesis","Formation of liquid endosperm in coconut"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: Meiosis forms haploid gametes, so in both plants and animals it occurs during gametogenesis.\nGrowth of root and shoot apices and repair of the gut lining both happen by mitosis.\nCoconut liquid endosperm forms by free-nuclear division: karyokinesis is not followed by cytokinesis. This is not meiosis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (phase) with List-II (characteristic event):\nList-I\nA. G1 phase\nB. S phase\nC. G2 phase\nD. M phase\nList-II\n(i) Proteins are synthesised in preparation for mitosis\n(ii) Cell is metabolically active and grows but does not replicate DNA\n(iii) Centriole duplicates in the cytoplasm of animal cells\n(iv) Karyokinesis usually followed by cytokinesis\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. G$_1$ → (ii): the cell is metabolically active and grows, but its DNA is not replicated yet.\nB. S → (iii): along with DNA replication, the centriole duplicates in the cytoplasm of animal cells.\nC. G$_2$ → (i): proteins are made in preparation for mitosis while growth continues.\nD. M → (iv): karyokinesis is usually followed by cytokinesis.\nDo not swap G$_1$ and G$_2$. G$_1$ is the growth phase before DNA replication.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Mitosis occurs exclusively in diploid cells in all living organisms.\nStatement II: In some lower plants, haploid cells divide by mitosis.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Mitosis is usually seen in diploid cells, but not exclusively. Male honey bees are haploid and still divide mitotically.\nStatement II is correct. In some lower plants, haploid cells divide by mitosis, as NCERT notes.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"During metaphase of mitosis, small disc-shaped structures on the surface of the centromere act as attachment sites for spindle fibres. These structures are called:","image":"","options":["Centrioles","Asters","Kinetochores","Chromomeres"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): Small disc-shaped structures on the surface of the centromere are called kinetochores. They serve as the sites where spindle fibres attach to chromosomes.\nSo (3) fits.\nCentrioles are found in the centrosome and help organise the spindle; they are not on the centromere.\nAsters are microtubules radiating from each centrosome.\nChromomeres are bead-like condensed regions along a chromatin thread.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In human cells in culture with a cell cycle of about 24 hours, cell division proper lasts only about an hour.\nStatement II: Interphase is called the resting phase because the cell shows neither cell growth nor DNA replication during it.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: in a 24-hour human cell cycle, M phase lasts only about an hour. Interphase therefore takes more than 95% of the cycle.\nStatement II is incorrect: interphase is called the resting phase, but the cell is not inactive.\nDuring interphase the cell grows (G1 and G2) and replicates its DNA (S phase) in an orderly way to prepare for division.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The beginning of diplotene is recognised by dissolution of the synaptonemal complex.\nStatement II: Crossing over in pachytene occurs between sister chromatids of the same chromosome.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Diplotene begins when the synaptonemal complex dissolves.\nStatement II is incorrect. Crossing over takes place between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes. It does not occur between sister chromatids.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Calling interphase the 'resting phase' is misleading.\nReason (R): During interphase the cell prepares for division by growing and replicating its DNA in an orderly manner.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: interphase is often called the 'resting phase', but the cell is highly active during it.\nR is true: during interphase the cell grows and replicates its DNA in an orderly way, preparing for division.\nR explains A: because so much preparation happens in interphase, the word 'resting' is a misnomer.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Meiosis halves the chromosome number, yet each sexually reproducing species keeps a fixed chromosome number from one generation to the next. Which event, together with meiosis, makes this possible?","image":"","options":["DNA replication during interkinesis","Fusion of two haploid gametes during fertilisation","Mitotic division of haploid gametes","Crossing over during pachytene"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Meiosis makes haploid (n) gametes. Fertilisation fuses two of them, which restores the diploid (2n) number in the zygote.\nThis cycle of halving and then restoring keeps the chromosome number constant across generations.\nThere is no DNA replication in interkinesis. Crossing over at pachytene reshuffles genes but does not change chromosome number. Mitosis of gametes keeps them haploid.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"During anaphase-I of meiosis","image":"","options":["Homologous chromosomes separate","Non-homologous chromosomes separate","Sister chromatids separate","Non-sister chromatids separate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In anaphase I the chiasmata resolve. Each homologous chromosome of a bivalent moves to an opposite pole.\nSister chromatids stay attached at their centromere, so each pole receives chromosomes that still have two chromatids.\nOption (3) is the common trap: sister chromatids separate only in anaphase II, as in mitosis.\nNon-homologous chromosomes are never paired, so they cannot 'separate' from each other.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 10 (MCQ Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"The M phase of the cell cycle begins with which event?","image":"","options":["Division of the cytoplasm (cytokinesis)","Replication of DNA","Nuclear division, in which the daughter chromosomes separate (karyokinesis)","Duplication of the centriole"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT: M phase starts with nuclear division (karyokinesis), in which the daughter chromosomes separate.\nIt usually ends with division of the cytoplasm (cytokinesis), so option (1) describes the end, not the start.\nDNA replication and centriole duplication happen earlier, in S phase of interphase.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following events does NOT occur during telophase of mitosis?","image":"","options":["Chromosomes decondense at the poles","A nuclear envelope develops around each chromosome cluster","The nucleolus, Golgi complex and ER reform","Spindle fibres attach to the kinetochores of chromosomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Telophase events (NCERT):\n- Chromosomes reach the poles and decondense, so (1) occurs.\n- A nuclear envelope forms around each chromosome cluster, so (2) occurs.\n- The nucleolus, Golgi complex and ER reform, so (3) occurs.\nAttachment of spindle fibres to kinetochores happens in metaphase, before the chromatids separate. So (4) does NOT occur in telophase.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the later stages of mitotic karyokinesis:\nI. At the onset of anaphase, the chromosomes at the metaphase plate split one after another, beginning at one end of the plate.\nII. In telophase, chromosomes that reach the poles decondense and lose their identity as discrete elements.\nIII. A nuclear envelope forms around the chromosome cluster at each pole during telophase.\nIV. The Golgi complex and endoplasmic reticulum reappear during anaphase, before the nuclear envelope forms.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"I. Incorrect: at the onset of anaphase all centromeres split at the same time, not one after another.\nII. Correct: in telophase the chromosomes decondense and lose their individuality.\nIII. Correct: a nuclear envelope forms around the chromosome cluster at each pole.\nIV. Incorrect: the Golgi complex, ER and nucleolus reform in telophase, not in anaphase.\nTwo statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (substage of prophase I) with List-II (characteristic event):\nList-I\nA. Leptotene\nB. Zygotene\nC. Diplotene\nD. Diakinesis\nList-II\nI. Dissolution of synaptonemal complex\nII. Terminalisation of chiasmata\nIII. Chromosomes become gradually visible under light microscope\nIV. Formation of synaptonemal complex\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Leptotene: chromosomes become gradually visible under the light microscope, so A matches III.\nB. Zygotene: synapsis occurs and the synaptonemal complex forms, so B matches IV.\nC. Diplotene: the synaptonemal complex dissolves, so C matches I.\nD. Diakinesis: chiasmata terminalise, so D matches II.\nWatch the trap: the synaptonemal complex forms in zygotene but dissolves in diplotene.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which organism does NCERT cite as completing its cell cycle in only about 90 minutes?","image":"","options":["Onion root tip cells","Human cells in culture","Yeast","Male honey bee"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Cell cycle duration differs between organisms.\nNCERT states that yeast can complete its cell cycle in about 90 minutes.\nHuman cells in culture take about 24 hours, so option (2) is wrong.\nOnion root tip cells and the male honey bee are not given as 90-minute examples.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about cytokinesis is incorrect?","image":"","options":["In animal cells, a furrow in the plasma membrane deepens and divides the cytoplasm into two.","In plant cells, new wall formation begins at the periphery and grows inward towards the centre.","The cell-plate represents the middle lamella between the walls of two adjacent cells.","Organelles like mitochondria and plastids get distributed between the two daughter cells during cytoplasmic division."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: animal cells divide by a furrow in the plasma membrane that deepens and splits the cytoplasm.\n(2) Incorrect: in plant cells the new wall begins as a cell plate at the centre and grows outward to meet the lateral walls. Inward growth from the periphery describes the animal furrow.\n(3) Correct: the cell plate becomes the middle lamella between the walls of the two cells.\n(4) Correct: organelles such as mitochondria and plastids are shared between the daughter cells.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"An onion root tip cell has 16 chromosomes (2n = 16), and its DNA content after M phase is 2C. During anaphase of mitosis, before cytokinesis, the whole cell contains:","image":"","options":["16 chromosomes and 4C DNA","32 chromosomes and 4C DNA","32 chromosomes and 8C DNA","16 chromosomes and 2C DNA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $2n = 16$, and the DNA content after M phase is 2C.\nIn S phase, DNA doubles to 4C. Each of the 16 chromosomes now has two sister chromatids.\nIn anaphase the centromeres split. The 32 chromatids become 32 separate daughter chromosomes. No new DNA is made and none is lost before cytokinesis.\nSo the whole cell has 32 chromosomes and 4C DNA.\nOption (3) is wrong because 8C would need a second round of replication, which does not happen.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nA. Meiosis\nB. Fertilisation\nC. S phase preceding meiosis I\nD. Recombination\nList-II\nI. Restores the diploid phase\nII. Produces identical sister chromatids\nIII. Ensures the haploid phase in the life cycle\nIV. Exchange between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A – III: meiosis halves the chromosome number, so it ensures the haploid phase.\nB – I: fertilisation fuses two haploid gametes, which restores the diploid phase.\nC – II: DNA replication in the S phase forms two identical sister chromatids per chromosome.\nD – IV: recombination is the exchange of segments between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"The M phase of the cell cycle begins with ______ and usually ends with ______.","image":"","options":["cytokinesis; karyokinesis","DNA replication; karyokinesis","karyokinesis; cytokinesis","centriole duplication; cytokinesis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"M phase starts with karyokinesis, the division of the nucleus, in which the daughter chromosomes separate.\nIt usually ends with cytokinesis, the division of the cytoplasm.\nDNA replication and centriole duplication are wrong choices because both happen earlier, in the S phase of interphase.\nOption (1) gives the right events but in reverse order.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In mitotic anaphase, a daughter chromosome moving towards a spindle pole is seen with one particular region at its leading edge while the chromosome arms trail behind. Which region leads?","image":"","options":["Telomere","Centromere","Nucleolar organiser region","Satellite"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Spindle fibres attach to the kinetochores, which sit on the centromere.\nIn anaphase each daughter chromosome is pulled towards its pole by the centromere. So the centromere leads and the arms trail behind.\nTelomeres are the tempting option, but they lie at the ends of the arms. They trail behind rather than lead.\nThe NOR and the satellite are not where spindle fibres attach.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Interphase is often called the 'resting phase'. Why is this name misleading?","image":"","options":["Chromosomes are fully condensed and clearly visible during interphase","The cell is actively preparing for division by growing and replicating its DNA","The nuclear envelope is absent throughout interphase","Cytokinesis takes place during interphase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: interphase is the phase between two M phases, when the cell prepares for division.\nDuring G$_1$ the cell grows, in S it replicates its DNA, and in G$_2$ it makes proteins for mitosis. So it is far from resting, which is why option (2) fits.\nOptions (1), (3) and (4) describe M phase events: chromosome condensation, loss of the nuclear envelope and cytokinesis. None of these happens in interphase.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A diploid cell with 2n = 8 has a DNA content of 2C in G1 and undergoes meiosis. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["Eight bivalents line up on the equatorial plate at metaphase I","Each cell at the end of telophase I has 4 chromosomes, each made of two chromatids, with a DNA content of 2C","DNA replicates during interkinesis, so each cell enters meiosis II with a DNA content of 4C","Each of the four haploid cells formed at the end of meiosis II contains 8 chromatids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: 8 chromosomes pair up into only 4 bivalents.\n(2) Correct: anaphase I sends one homologue of each pair, still with two chromatids, to each pole. Each telophase I cell therefore has 4 chromosomes and half of 4C, i.e. 2C DNA.\n(3) Incorrect: interkinesis is short and has no DNA replication, so cells enter meiosis II with 2C.\n(4) Incorrect: each final haploid cell has 4 single-chromatid chromosomes (4 chromatids) and C DNA.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The duration of the cell cycle is the same for all cell types within a given organism.\nStatement II: The events by which replicated chromosomes are distributed to daughter nuclei are under genetic control.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Cell cycle duration varies between organisms (yeast about 90 min, human cells about 24 h). It also varies between cell types within the same organism.\nStatement II is correct. DNA synthesis and the distribution of replicated chromosomes to daughter nuclei are under genetic control.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which event marks the beginning of anaphase II?","image":"","options":["Simultaneous splitting of the centromere of each chromosome","Dissolution of the synaptonemal complex","Terminalisation of chiasmata","Reappearance of the nuclear envelope around chromosome groups"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: anaphase II begins with the simultaneous splitting of the centromere of each chromosome.\nThe centromere had been holding the sister chromatids together. After it splits, the chromatids move to opposite poles as the kinetochore microtubules shorten.\nDissolution of the synaptonemal complex (diplotene) and terminalisation of chiasmata (diakinesis) both belong to prophase I.\nReappearance of the nuclear envelope happens in telophase II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Meiosis occurs in organisms during","image":"","options":["Sexual reproduction","Vegetative reproduction","Both sexual and vegetative reproduction","None of the above"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Meiosis produces haploid gametes. Their fusion at fertilisation restores the diploid number, so meiosis is an essential part of sexual reproduction.\nVegetative reproduction forms new plants from roots, stems or leaves only by mitosis. The offspring are genetically identical clones and no gametes are involved.\nOption (3) fails because vegetative propagation involves no meiosis.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 10 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In human cells growing in culture, interphase takes up more than 95% of the cell cycle.\nStatement II: Yeast cells take about 24 hours to complete one cell cycle.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A human cell in culture divides roughly once in 24 hours. M phase lasts only about 1 hour, so interphase takes up more than 95% of the cycle.\nStatement II is incorrect. Yeast completes its cell cycle in only about 90 minutes. The 24-hour figure applies to human cells.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A diploid cell with 2n = 20 undergoes three successive rounds of mitotic karyokinesis, each preceded by normal DNA replication. None of these rounds is followed by cytokinesis. What is the result?","image":"","options":["Eight separate cells, each with 20 chromosomes","One cell with 8 nuclei, each nucleus having 20 chromosomes","One cell with 6 nuclei, each nucleus having 10 chromosomes","One cell with a single nucleus having 160 chromosomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 2n = 20; three mitotic karyokineses; no cytokinesis.\nEach mitotic nuclear division doubles the number of nuclei: $1 \\to 2 \\to 4 \\to 8$. This equals $2^3 = 8$ nuclei.\nMitosis is equational, so each nucleus keeps 20 chromosomes.\nWithout cytokinesis the cytoplasm never divides. The result is one multinucleate cell (a syncytium) containing 8 nuclei.\nOption (4) is wrong because karyokinesis does produce separate nuclei.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) The chromosome number per cell is reduced from 2n to n by meiosis I.\n(b) The DNA content per cell is halved again during meiosis II.\n(c) DNA replicates during interkinesis to prepare for meiosis II.\n(d) The chromosome number per cell doubles during S phase.\n(e) Meiosis II reduces the chromosome number from n to n/2.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct. Meiosis I separates homologues, so the number goes from 2n to n.\n(b) Correct. Meiosis II halves the DNA again, from 2C to C.\n(c) Incorrect. No DNA replication takes place during interkinesis.\n(d) Incorrect. S phase doubles the DNA but not the chromosome number.\n(e) Incorrect. Meiosis II keeps the number at n and separates sister chromatids.\nTwo statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A somatic cell of a male honey bee contains 16 chromosomes and is haploid. If this cell divides by mitosis, the daughter cells will have","image":"","options":["8 chromosomes each, genetically different from the parent","16 chromosomes each, genetically identical to the parent","32 chromosomes each, as mitosis produces diploid cells","16 chromosomes each, but genetically different due to crossing over"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: Mitosis is an equational division. Daughter cells keep the same chromosome number and the same genes as the parent, whatever its ploidy.\nApplying it: the haploid parent has 16 chromosomes, so each daughter cell also gets 16 identical chromosomes.\n(1) is wrong because halving happens only in meiosis.\n(3) is wrong because mitosis does not convert haploid cells into diploid ones.\n(4) is wrong because crossing over is a meiotic (prophase I) event, not a mitotic one.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"During cytokinesis in a plant cell, in which direction does formation of the new wall proceed?","image":"","options":["From the existing lateral walls inward to the centre","From one pole of the cell towards the other pole","From the centre of the cell outward to meet the existing lateral walls","Simultaneously along the entire equatorial plane at once"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Plant cells have a rigid wall, so they cannot form a furrow.\nWall formation starts at the centre of the cell as the cell plate. It grows outward until it meets the existing lateral walls.\nOption (1), inward from the periphery, is the trap. That direction describes the cleavage furrow of animal cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A bivalent of meiosis-I consists of","image":"","options":["Two chromatids and one centromere","Two chromatids and two centromere","Four chromatids and two centromere","Four chromatids and four centromere"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A bivalent (tetrad) is a pair of homologous chromosomes that synapse during zygotene of prophase I.\nEach homologue has already replicated, so it carries 2 sister chromatids joined at 1 centromere.\nTwo homologues therefore give $2 \\\\times 2 = 4$ chromatids and $2 \\\\times 1 = 2$ centromeres.\nOption (4) is wrong because sister chromatids share one centromere until anaphase II.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 10 (MCQ Q6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In a typical human cell in culture with a cell cycle of about 24 hours, roughly what fraction of the cycle is spent in interphase?","image":"","options":["About 50%","About 75%","More than 95%","Less than 5%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: a human cell in culture divides about once every 24 hours.\nFact: the actual M phase (cell division proper) lasts only about 1 hour.\nSo interphase takes up more than 95% of the cycle.\nTrap: 'less than 5%' is the share of M phase, not of interphase.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about prophase I are correct?\n(a) Compaction of chromosomes continues throughout leptotene.\n(b) Crossing over takes place between sister chromatids of the same chromosome.\n(c) Recombination between homologous chromosomes is completed by the end of pachytene.\n(d) The nuclear envelope breaks down by the end of diplotene.\n(e) Leptotene and zygotene are short-lived compared to pachytene.\n(f) Chiasmata are X-shaped structures.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: chromosome compaction continues throughout leptotene.\n(b) Incorrect: crossing over occurs between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes, not between sister chromatids.\n(c) Correct: recombination is completed by the end of pachytene.\n(d) Incorrect: the nuclear envelope breaks down by the end of diakinesis, not diplotene.\n(e) Correct: leptotene and zygotene are short-lived compared with pachytene.\n(f) Correct: chiasmata are X-shaped structures.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Under the microscope, a dividing cell shows two daughter nuclei, each with a nuclear envelope and a nucleolus. A cell plate is visible at the centre of the cell but has not yet reached the side walls. Which interpretation is correct?","image":"","options":["It is an animal cell in late anaphase, with karyokinesis still in progress","It is a plant cell in which karyokinesis is complete and cytokinesis is in progress","It is a plant cell forming a syncytium, because the cytoplasm will not divide","It is a plant cell in which cytokinesis is complete but karyokinesis is incomplete"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A reformed nuclear envelope and nucleolus mark the end of telophase, so karyokinesis is complete.\nA cell plate is unique to plant cells. It forms at the centre and grows outward to the lateral walls. Since it has not yet reached the walls, cytokinesis is still in progress.\nOption (1) fails because animal cells form a furrow, not a cell plate.\nOption (3) fails because a growing cell plate shows that the cytoplasm is dividing.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In plant cells, cytokinesis begins with the formation of a simple precursor structure that later represents the middle lamella between the walls of two adjacent daughter cells. This precursor is called the:","image":"","options":["Cleavage furrow","Cell plate","Phragmoplast-free septum","Metaphase plate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In plant cells, wall formation starts at the centre with a simple precursor called the cell plate. It grows outward to meet the lateral walls and later represents the middle lamella between the two daughter cells.\nA cleavage furrow is the animal-cell mechanism, which a rigid cell wall prevents.\nThe metaphase plate is only the plane where chromosomes align during metaphase.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Kinetochore\nB. Synaptonemal complex\nC. Recombination nodule\nD. Cell plate\nList-II\ni. Precursor representing the middle lamella\nii. Site of crossing over between non-sister chromatids\niii. Disc-shaped structure where spindle fibres attach\niv. Complex structure accompanying synapsis\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A. Kinetochore: a disc-shaped structure at the centromere where spindle fibres attach, which is iii.\nB. Synaptonemal complex: the complex structure that accompanies synapsis, which is iv.\nC. Recombination nodule: the site of crossing over between non-sister chromatids, which is ii.\nD. Cell plate: the precursor of the middle lamella, which is i.\nOption (1) is tempting but swaps A and B.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about meiosis is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["No DNA replication occurs during interkinesis.","In anaphase I, homologous chromosomes separate while sister chromatids remain associated at their centromeres.","In oocytes of some vertebrates, diplotene can last for months or years.","Recombination between homologous chromosomes is completed only by the end of diplotene, when chiasmata appear."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: interkinesis is short, and DNA is not replicated during it.\n(2) Correct: in anaphase I, homologues separate while sister chromatids stay joined at their centromeres.\n(3) Correct: in oocytes of some vertebrates, diplotene can last for months or years.\n(4) Incorrect: recombination (crossing over) is completed by the end of pachytene. In diplotene the synaptonemal complex dissolves, and chiasmata simply become visible as the points where homologues remain linked.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"The G1 phase of the cell cycle corresponds to the interval between:","image":"","options":["DNA replication and the start of mitosis","Mitosis and the initiation of DNA replication","Prophase and telophase","Karyokinesis and cytokinesis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: G1 (Gap 1) is the interval between mitosis and the start of DNA replication.\nThe order is M → G1 → S → G2 → M, so G1 sits between the end of mitosis and the S phase.\nOption (1) describes the interval between DNA replication and mitosis, which is G2, not G1.\nOptions (3) and (4) are stages within M phase, not gap phases of interphase.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about cytokinesis is incorrect?","image":"","options":["In animal cells, a furrow in the plasma membrane deepens and divides the cytoplasm into two","In plant cells, the cell-plate begins at the periphery of the cell and grows inward to meet in the centre","The cell-plate represents the middle lamella between the walls of two adjacent cells","Karyokinesis without cytokinesis produces a multinucleate syncytium, as in the liquid endosperm of coconut"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: in animal cells, a furrow in the plasma membrane deepens and splits the cytoplasm.\n(2) Incorrect: in plant cells, the cell-plate begins in the centre and grows outward to meet the existing lateral walls. Inward growth from the periphery is the animal furrow pattern.\n(3) Correct: the cell-plate becomes the middle lamella between adjacent cell walls.\n(4) Correct: karyokinesis without cytokinesis gives a multinucleate syncytium, as in the liquid endosperm of coconut.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Heart cells of an adult animal can be regarded as cells that have exited the active cell cycle.\nReason (R): Cells in the quiescent stage have permanently stopped all metabolic activity.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Adult heart cells do not appear to divide, so they are regarded as having exited the active cell cycle (they stay in $G_0$).\nR is false. Quiescent ($G_0$) cells remain metabolically active. They only stop proliferating and can divide again if required.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"NCERT states that M phase begins with karyokinesis and 'usually' ends with cytokinesis. Which observation best justifies the word 'usually'?","image":"","options":["Plant cells form a cell plate instead of a cleavage furrow","In the liquid endosperm of coconut, nuclear division is not followed by cytoplasmic division, producing a multinucleate syncytium","Heart cells of adult animals exit G1 and enter the G0 stage","Yeast completes its cell cycle in only about 90 minutes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: karyokinesis is not always followed by cytokinesis.\nIn the liquid endosperm of coconut, nuclei divide repeatedly without the cytoplasm dividing. This produces a multinucleate syncytium, so M phase does not always end with cytokinesis.\nOption (1) is wrong: forming a cell plate is still cytokinesis, done differently in plants.\nOption (3) is wrong: G0 cells do not divide at all.\nOption (4) is wrong: yeast's 90-minute cycle concerns how long the cycle takes, not cytokinesis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Asters\n(b) Centromere\n(c) Metaphase plate\n(d) Mitotic apparatus\nList-II\n(i) Plane in which chromosomes align at the cell equator\n(ii) Asters together with spindle fibres\n(iii) Microtubules radiating from each centrosome\n(iv) Region holding the two sister chromatids together","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Asters are microtubules radiating from each centrosome → (iii).\n(b) The centromere is the region holding the two sister chromatids together → (iv).\n(c) The metaphase plate is the plane in which chromosomes align at the equator → (i).\n(d) The mitotic apparatus is the asters together with the spindle fibres → (ii).\nThe correct matching is a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The two daughter chromosomes that move to opposite poles from a single chromosome during anaphase II need not be genetically identical.\nStatement II: During pachytene of prophase I, crossing over exchanges genetic material between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct: at pachytene, crossing over exchanges segments between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes.\nStatement I is correct: because of crossing over, the two sister chromatids of one chromosome can carry different allele combinations.\nSo the daughter chromosomes separated in anaphase II need not be genetically identical. This differs from mitosis, where they are identical copies.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Interphase is often called the resting phase of the cell cycle.\nReason (R): During interphase the cell stops all metabolic activity and waits for the next division.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: interphase is often called the resting phase.\nR is false: interphase is not a true rest. During interphase the cell is metabolically active and prepares for division by growing and replicating its DNA in an orderly way.\nSo A is true and R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"As a cell grows in size, which ratio gets disturbed, making cell division necessary to restore it?","image":"","options":["Ratio between DNA and RNA","Ratio between nucleus and cytoplasm","Ratio between mitochondria and plastids","Ratio between cell wall and plasma membrane"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"As a cell grows, its cytoplasm increases much more than its nucleus.\nThis disturbs the nucleo-cytoplasmic ratio, so the nucleus can no longer control the larger cytoplasm efficiently.\nCell division restores this ratio, which NCERT lists as a significance of mitosis.\nThe DNA:RNA ratio and the other options are not the ratios that growth upsets.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Cells which are not dividing are likely to be at","image":"","options":["G$_1$","G$_2$","G$_0$","S phase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Some cells, such as many cells of adult heart and nerve tissue, stop dividing. They exit $G_1$ and enter an inactive stage called the quiescent stage, $G_0$.\nIn $G_0$ they stay metabolically active but do not proliferate unless the organism needs them to.\nOption (1) is tempting, but $G_1$ cells are still in the cycle and preparing for DNA replication.\n$G_2$ and S phase belong to cells that are actively cycling.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 10 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"During anaphase I of meiosis, which structures move towards opposite poles of the cell?","image":"","options":["Sister chromatids, after the centromere splits","Homologous chromosomes, each still made of two sister chromatids joined at the centromere","Non-sister chromatids, after the recombination nodules are formed","Individual chromatids of a bivalent, four going to each pole"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): in anaphase I, homologous chromosomes separate, while sister chromatids remain associated at their centromeres.\nSo each pole receives whole chromosomes, each still made of two chromatids. This fits option (2).\nOption (1) is the tempting trap: centromeres split and sister chromatids separate only in anaphase II, not anaphase I.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Recombination nodules, the sites where crossing over takes place, first appear during which substage of prophase I?","image":"","options":["Zygotene","Pachytene","Diplotene","Diakinesis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Recombination nodules appear during pachytene. They are the sites where non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes exchange segments, with the help of the enzyme recombinase.\nZygotene is the tempting wrong option. It involves synapsis and formation of the synaptonemal complex, but crossing over has not yet occurred.\nChiasmata, the visible result of crossing over, appear later in diplotene.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) A cell in G1 phase is metabolically active and grows continuously.\n(b) DNA replication takes place during G1 phase.\n(c) Proteins required for mitosis are synthesised during G2 phase.\n(d) Cell growth stops completely once a cell enters G2 phase.\n(e) The chromosome number of a cell doubles during S phase.","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: a G1 cell is metabolically active and grows continuously.\n(b) Incorrect: DNA replicates in S phase, not in G1.\n(c) Correct: proteins needed for mitosis are made in G2.\n(d) Incorrect: cell growth continues during G2.\n(e) Incorrect: only the DNA content doubles in S phase; the chromosome number stays 2n.\nSo two statements, (a) and (c), are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following structures reappear or reform during telophase of mitosis?\nNuclear envelope, Nucleolus, Golgi complex, Endoplasmic reticulum, Synaptonemal complex, Metaphase plate","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Structures that reform in telophase (NCERT):\n- Nuclear envelope: reforms.\n- Nucleolus: reforms.\n- Golgi complex: reforms.\n- Endoplasmic reticulum: reforms.\nSynaptonemal complex: does not count. It forms only in meiotic prophase I, not in mitosis.\nMetaphase plate: does not count. It is just the plane where chromosomes align at metaphase, not a structure that reforms.\nCount = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A plant has 2n = 24. How many chromosomes and how many chromatids does a cell of its root tip have in G2 phase?","image":"","options":["48 chromosomes, 48 chromatids","24 chromosomes, 24 chromatids","12 chromosomes, 24 chromatids","24 chromosomes, 48 chromatids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A root tip cell divides by mitosis, so it is diploid: $2n = 24$.\nS phase replicates DNA but does not add centromeres. In G2 the cell therefore still has 24 chromosomes.\nEach chromosome now has two sister chromatids: $24 \\times 2 = 48$ chromatids.\nOption (1) wrongly doubles the chromosome count, and (3) wrongly halves it.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (sub-stages of prophase I) with List-II (characteristic events):\nList-I\nA. Leptotene\nB. Zygotene\nC. Pachytene\nD. Diplotene\nList-II\nI. Appearance of recombination nodules\nII. Chromosomes become gradually visible under the light microscope\nIII. Dissolution of synaptonemal complex and appearance of chiasmata\nIV. Synapsis accompanied by formation of synaptonemal complex","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Leptotene: chromosomes become gradually visible under the light microscope (II).\nB. Zygotene: synapsis occurs, with formation of the synaptonemal complex (IV).\nC. Pachytene: recombination nodules appear, marking the sites of crossing over (I).\nD. Diplotene: the synaptonemal complex dissolves and X-shaped chiasmata appear (III).\nSo the correct matching is A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A diploid plant cell has 24 chromosomes and 2C DNA in G1 phase. What will be its chromosome number and DNA content at the end of S phase?","image":"","options":["48 chromosomes and 4C","24 chromosomes and 4C","24 chromosomes and 2C","12 chromosomes and 2C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: a diploid cell in G1 has 2n = 24 chromosomes and 2C DNA.\nIn S phase, DNA replicates, so the DNA content doubles from 2C to 4C.\nEach chromosome now has two sister chromatids joined at a single centromere.\nSo the chromosome number stays at 24.\nAt the end of S phase the cell therefore has 24 chromosomes and 4C DNA. Option (1) wrongly doubles the chromosome number.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A cell from an organism with 2n = 6 is observed under the microscope. It shows three chromosomes lined up singly at the equator, and no homologous pairs are present. Each chromosome consists of two chromatids, and the kinetochores of sister chromatids are attached to spindle fibres from opposite poles. The cell is most likely in:","image":"","options":["Metaphase of mitosis","Metaphase I of meiosis","Metaphase II of meiosis","Anaphase II of meiosis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 2n = 6. The cell shows 3 (= n) unpaired chromosomes, each with two chromatids, and sister kinetochores attached to opposite poles.\nMitotic metaphase fails: it would show all 6 chromosomes, including both members of each homologous pair.\nMetaphase I fails: it would show bivalents, i.e. paired homologues.\nAnaphase II fails: sister chromatids would already be moving apart.\nA haploid set of single two-chromatid chromosomes at the equator fits metaphase II.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about cytokinesis in plant cells is correct?","image":"","options":["A furrow in the plasma membrane deepens from the periphery towards the centre","Wall formation starts at the existing lateral walls and grows inward","The cell-plate formed in the centre represents the middle lamella between the two daughter cells","Organelles like mitochondria and plastids stay in only one of the daughter cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: furrowing from the periphery inward happens in animal cells.\n(2) Incorrect: a plant cell has a rigid wall, so the new wall starts at the centre and grows outward to meet the lateral walls.\n(3) Correct: the cell-plate formed at the centre becomes the middle lamella between the two daughter cells.\n(4) Incorrect: mitochondria and plastids are distributed to both daughter cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Interphase lasts more than 95% of the duration of the cell cycle in human cells.\n(ii) The number of chromosomes doubles during S phase.\n(iii) In animal cells, the centriole duplicates in the cytoplasm during S phase.\n(iv) Cells in the G0 stage are metabolically inactive.\n(v) During G2 phase, proteins are synthesised in preparation for mitosis.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: in human cells, interphase takes more than 95% of the cycle.\n(ii) Incorrect: in S phase the DNA content doubles from 2C to 4C, but the chromosome number stays 2n.\n(iii) Correct: in animal cells the centriole duplicates in the cytoplasm during S phase.\n(iv) Incorrect: G0 cells are metabolically active; they simply stop proliferating.\n(v) Correct: proteins needed for mitosis are made during G2.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A dividing animal cell (2n) is observed under the microscope. Two masses of chromatin lie at opposite poles, and individual chromosomes cannot be made out. A nuclear envelope is forming around each mass, and no cleavage furrow has appeared yet. Which statement about this cell is correct?","image":"","options":["It is in late prophase, because individual chromosomes are not yet distinct.","Each chromatin mass carries the parental chromosome number (2n), each chromosome consisting of a single chromatid.","The nucleolus is absent and will reappear only after cytokinesis is complete.","It is in early anaphase, because the centromeres have just split."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Two chromatin masses at opposite poles, with nuclear envelopes reforming and no furrow yet, means the cell is in telophase.\nEach pole received one daughter chromosome from every original chromosome. So each mass is 2n, and each chromosome is a single chromatid. This is why mitosis is called equational.\n(1) Wrong: in prophase the chromosomes condense inside one nucleus. They do not form two masses.\n(3) Wrong: the nucleolus reappears during telophase itself, not after cytokinesis.\n(4) Wrong: anaphase shows distinct chromosomes moving apart, not decondensed masses inside new envelopes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the events listed below is not observed during mitosis?","image":"","options":["Chromatin condensation","Movement of centrioles to opposite poles","Appearance of chromosomes with two chromatids joined together at the centromere.","Crossing over"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Observed: chromatin condenses into chromosomes in prophase.\n(2) Observed: in animal cells, centrioles move to opposite poles in prophase and organise the spindle.\n(3) Observed: chromosomes appear with two sister chromatids joined at the centromere.\n(4) Not observed: crossing over is exchange between non-sister chromatids of homologues. It needs synapsis and happens at pachytene of meiosis I, never in mitosis.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 10 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In a dividing plant cell, the cell plate formed during cytokinesis represents the precursor of which structure?","image":"","options":["Primary cell wall","Plasma membrane","Middle lamella","Secondary cell wall"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In plant cells, cytokinesis begins with a simple precursor called the cell plate.\nThe cell plate represents the middle lamella between the walls of the two daughter cells.\nThe primary wall, and later the secondary wall, are laid down on either side of it afterwards. That is why options (1) and (4) are wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Meiosis increases genetic variability in a population from one generation to the next.\nStatement II: During pachytene, crossing over between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes leads to recombination of genetic material.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Meiosis increases genetic variability from one generation to the next, and this variation matters for evolution.\nStatement II is correct. At pachytene, crossing over between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes exchanges segments and so recombines genetic material.\nCrossing over is one source of the variation described in Statement I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Cell growth\n(B) DNA synthesis\n(C) Karyokinesis\n(D) Cytokinesis\nList-II\n(I) Separation of daughter chromosomes into nuclei\n(II) Occurs only during one specific stage of the cycle\n(III) Division of cytoplasm, usually ending M phase\n(IV) Continuous process throughout the cycle","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A – IV: cell growth (increase in cytoplasm) continues throughout the cycle.\nB – II: DNA synthesis happens only in one stage (S phase).\nC – I: karyokinesis separates the daughter chromosomes into nuclei.\nD – III: cytokinesis divides the cytoplasm and usually ends M phase.\nThis matching is A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III, which is option (1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about interkinesis is correct?","image":"","options":["DNA replicates during interkinesis so that each chromatid can be copied before meiosis II","Interkinesis is generally short-lived, and no DNA replication occurs during it","During interkinesis the chromosomes always return to the fully extended state of an interphase nucleus","Interkinesis is followed by prophase II, which is longer and more complex than prophase I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes interkinesis as the generally short-lived stage between meiosis I and meiosis II, with no DNA replication. This matches option (2).\nOption (1) is wrong because no replication occurs in interkinesis.\nOption (3) is wrong because the chromosomes usually do not fully elongate; meiosis II often begins before that.\nOption (4) is wrong because prophase II is simpler than prophase I, not longer or more complex.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"During which substage of prophase I do homologous chromosomes begin to pair (synapsis)?","image":"","options":["Leptotene","Pachytene","Zygotene","Diplotene"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: prophase I has five substages: leptotene, zygotene, pachytene, diplotene and diakinesis.\nSynapsis, the pairing of homologous chromosomes, begins in zygotene. It is accompanied by formation of the synaptonemal complex, and the paired homologues are called bivalents.\nThe tempting option, pachytene, is the stage of crossing over, not the start of pairing.\nIn leptotene, chromosomes only become visible. In diplotene, the synaptonemal complex dissolves and chiasmata appear.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following do haploid cells divide by mitosis?","image":"","options":["Worker honey bee","Male honey bee (drone)","Queen honey bee","Human spermatogonia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In animals, mitosis usually occurs in diploid somatic cells.\nNCERT cites the male honey bee (drone) as an exception. Drones develop from unfertilised eggs, so they are haploid, and their cells divide by mitosis.\nWorker bees and queens develop from fertilised eggs, so they are diploid.\nHuman spermatogonia are also diploid ($2n = 46$).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In an animal cell, how is the cytoplasm divided into two during cytokinesis?","image":"","options":["A cell plate forms in the centre and grows towards the plasma membrane","A furrow appears in the plasma membrane, deepens and meets in the centre","Spindle fibres pull the plasma membrane apart at the equator","A new cell wall forms around each daughter nucleus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Animal cells have no cell wall. Cytokinesis starts as a furrow in the plasma membrane. The furrow deepens and finally meets in the centre, splitting the cytoplasm in two.\nOption (1) is the tempting wrong answer. A cell plate growing from the centre outward describes plant cells, which have a rigid wall.\nSpindle fibres do not pull the membrane apart, and animal cells form no wall.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"During zygotene, homologous chromosomes start pairing with each other. This process of association is termed:","image":"","options":["Synapsis","Terminalisation","Chiasma formation","Interkinesis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In zygotene, homologous chromosomes begin to pair, and this pairing is called synapsis.\nSynapsis is accompanied by the formation of the synaptonemal complex, which produces bivalents.\nChiasmata become visible only in diplotene, and terminalisation of chiasmata happens in diakinesis.\nInterkinesis is the short gap between meiosis I and meiosis II, so it is not part of prophase I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Apical meristem\n(B) Lateral cambium\n(C) Upper epidermal cells of skin\n(D) Male honey bee\nList-II\n(I) Mitosis in haploid cells\n(II) Increase in girth of the plant\n(III) Increase in length of root and shoot\n(IV) Constant replacement of cells (repair)","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–III: apical meristems at root and shoot tips increase length.\nB–II: lateral cambium increases girth.\nC–IV: upper epidermal cells of skin are constantly replaced by mitosis (repair).\nD–I: male honey bees are haploid, yet their cells divide mitotically.\nThis matches option (1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: During the S and G2 phases, the newly formed DNA molecules are already distinct and can be seen as separate chromatids.\nStatement II: During prophase, the chromosomal material becomes untangled as chromatin condensation proceeds.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. During S and G2 the new DNA molecules are not distinct; they are intertwined with each other.\nStatement II is correct. During prophase, the chromosomal material untangles as chromatin condenses into visible chromosomes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following cells are expected to undergo mitotic division?\n(i) Haploid cells of a moss gametophyte\n(ii) Diploid cells of an onion root tip\n(iii) Haploid body cells of a male honey bee\n(iv) Heart cells of an adult human","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: the haploid moss gametophyte grows by mitosis.\n(ii) Yes: diploid onion root tip cells are actively dividing meristematic cells.\n(iii) Yes: drone body cells are haploid but divide mitotically.\n(iv) No: adult heart cells do not appear to divide and remain in $G_0$.\nThree of the four cells divide by mitosis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A somatic cell has 2n = 24 chromosomes, and its DNA content in G1 is 2C. It completes one mitotic cell cycle. What are the chromosome number and DNA content per cell at G1, at G2, and in each daughter cell immediately after M phase, respectively?","image":"","options":["24, 2C; 48, 4C; 24, 2C","24, 2C; 24, 4C; 24, 2C","24, 2C; 24, 4C; 12, 1C","24, 4C; 24, 4C; 24, 2C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 2n = 24 and DNA = 2C in G$_1$.\nS phase doubles the DNA to 4C, but the chromosome number does not change. So G$_2$ has 24 chromosomes and 4C DNA.\nMitosis separates the sister chromatids equally. Each daughter cell gets 24 chromosomes and 2C DNA.\nOption (1) wrongly doubles the chromosome number. Option (3) halves it to 12, which happens in meiosis, not mitosis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The liquid endosperm of coconut is a multinucleate mass of cytoplasm.\nReason (R): In some organisms, karyokinesis is not followed by cytokinesis, which results in a syncytium.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The liquid endosperm of coconut is a multinucleate mass of cytoplasm.\nR is true. In some organisms karyokinesis is not followed by cytokinesis, and this produces a syncytium.\nR explains A. Coconut endosperm becomes multinucleate because its nuclei divide repeatedly while the cytoplasm does not. It is the standard NCERT example of a syncytium, not a result of cell fusion.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In meiosis, recombination of genetic material takes place between:","image":"","options":["Sister chromatids of the same chromosome","Non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes","Non-homologous chromosomes","Daughter chromosomes after anaphase II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: Homologous chromosomes pair (synapsis) during zygotene. Crossing over then occurs at pachytene between non-sister chromatids of the homologues.\nThis exchange combines maternal and paternal segments, which is genetic recombination.\n(1) is wrong because sister chromatids are identical copies, so swapping segments between them changes nothing.\n(3) is wrong because non-homologous chromosomes do not pair.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Anaphase I reduces the chromosome number of each daughter nucleus to half that of the parent cell.\nReason (R): In anaphase I, centromeres split and sister chromatids move to opposite poles.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: in anaphase I, whole homologous chromosomes separate to opposite poles. Each pole therefore receives half the parental chromosome number, which makes meiosis I the reductional division.\nR is false: in anaphase I the sister chromatids stay joined at their centromeres.\nCentromere splitting and separation of sister chromatids occur only in anaphase II.\nSo A is true but R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about meiosis is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Four cells arise from one meiocyte because two divisions follow a single round of DNA replication","If a gamete has DNA content C, the meiocyte just before the start of meiosis I has DNA content 2C","The zygote formed by fusion of two gametes, each with DNA content C, has DNA content 2C","Meiosis produces the haploid phase of the life cycle, while fertilisation restores the diploid phase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: one DNA replication followed by two divisions gives four cells.\n(2) Incorrect: a gamete with C comes from a meiocyte with 2C in G1. After the S phase this becomes 4C, so just before meiosis I the meiocyte has 4C, not 2C.\n(3) Correct: $C + C = 2C$ in the zygote.\n(4) Correct: meiosis gives the haploid phase, and fertilisation restores the diploid phase.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following structures are NOT visible in a cell at the end of mitotic prophase when viewed under the microscope?\nGolgi complex, endoplasmic reticulum, nucleolus, nuclear envelope, mitochondria, chromosomes with two chromatids","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"At the end of mitotic prophase, NCERT notes that the following are no longer seen:\n- Golgi complex\n- endoplasmic reticulum\n- nucleolus\n- nuclear envelope\nThat makes 4 structures that are not visible.\nMitochondria persist. Condensed chromosomes, each with two sister chromatids, are clearly visible.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following does karyokinesis occur without being followed by cytokinesis, producing a multinucleate syncytium?","image":"","options":["Cells of the onion root tip","Liquid endosperm of coconut","Cells lining the gut","Haploid cells of male honey bees"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Normally, karyokinesis (nuclear division) is followed by cytokinesis (division of the cytoplasm).\nIn the liquid endosperm of coconut, the nucleus divides repeatedly without the cytoplasm dividing. This produces a multinucleate condition called a syncytium.\nThe other options do not fit:\n• Onion root tip cells and gut-lining cells undergo normal mitosis with cytokinesis.\n• Haploid cells of male honey bees also divide by mitosis with cytokinesis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the cell cycle are correct?\n(i) In human cells in culture, interphase lasts more than 95% of the duration of the cell cycle.\n(ii) Cell growth in terms of cytoplasmic increase is restricted to the G1 phase.\n(iii) DNA synthesis occurs only during one specific stage of the cell cycle.\n(iv) Proteins needed for mitosis are synthesised during the G2 phase.\n(v) A cell in G1 phase is metabolically active but does not replicate its DNA.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: in a 24 h human cell cycle, mitosis takes only about 1 h, so interphase is more than 95% of the cycle.\n(ii) Incorrect: cytoplasmic growth is a continuous process, so it is not restricted to G$_1$.\n(iii) Correct: DNA synthesis happens only in the S phase.\n(iv) Correct: proteins needed for mitosis are made in G$_2$.\n(v) Correct: a G$_1$ cell is metabolically active but does not replicate its DNA.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"During anaphase I of meiosis, which of the following moves towards the opposite poles of the cell?","image":"","options":["Sister chromatids of each chromosome, after the centromere splits","Homologous chromosomes, each still made of two sister chromatids","Non-sister chromatids, released from the chiasmata","Entire bivalents, without separation of homologues"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: in anaphase I, the homologous chromosomes of each bivalent separate and move to opposite poles.\nThe sister chromatids remain attached at their centromeres, so each moving chromosome still has two chromatids.\nOption (1) is tempting, but centromere splitting and sister-chromatid separation happen in anaphase II (and in mitosis), not in anaphase I.\nOption (4) is wrong because the bivalents do split up; whole bivalents do not move to one pole.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about meiosis are correct?\n(a) Meiosis takes place in specialised diploid cells that give rise to gametes.\n(b) Recombination during meiosis occurs between sister chromatids of the same chromosome.\n(c) Meiosis I begins only after the parental chromosomes have replicated in the S phase to form identical sister chromatids.\n(d) Fertilisation restores the diploid phase in the life cycle.\n(e) Two haploid cells are formed at the end of meiosis II.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: meiosis occurs in specialised diploid cells that give rise to gametes.\n(b) Incorrect: recombination is between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes.\n(c) Correct: meiosis I follows the S phase, which forms identical sister chromatids.\n(d) Correct: fertilisation restores diploidy.\n(e) Incorrect: meiosis II ends with four haploid cells, not two.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Crossing over between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes during prophase I is an enzyme-mediated process. Which enzyme is involved?","image":"","options":["Ligase","Recombinase","Helicase","Topoisomerase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that crossing over during pachytene is an enzyme-mediated process. The enzyme involved is recombinase.\nIt mediates the exchange of genetic material between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes.\nHelicase and topoisomerase are linked mainly with unwinding DNA during replication, not with crossing over.\nLigase seals nicks in DNA, but it is not the enzyme NCERT names for crossing over.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): During S phase, the DNA content of a cell increases from 2C to 4C.\nReason (R): During S phase, the chromosome number of a diploid cell increases from 2n to 4n.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. DNA replication in S phase doubles the DNA content from 2C to 4C.\nR is false. Each chromosome now consists of two sister chromatids, but the chromosome number does not change. A diploid cell stays $2n$ after S phase.\nDoubling of DNA is not the same as doubling of chromosome number.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Meiosis results in diploid organisms","image":"","options":["Production of gametes","Reduction in the number of chromosomes","Introduction of variation","All of the above"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"In a diploid organism ($2n$), meiosis is the division that produces gametes.\n(1) Correct: it forms haploid gametes (or spores).\n(2) Correct: chromosome number is halved from $2n$ to $n$, hence 'reduction division'.\n(3) Correct: crossing over at pachytene and independent assortment of homologues at metaphase I create new gene combinations.\nAll three statements are true, so no single option alone is enough.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 10 (MCQ Q1)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a diploid cell, the number of chromosomes present in G2 phase is double the number present in G1 phase.\nStatement II: During S phase, the DNA content of a diploid cell increases from 2C to 4C.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. A diploid cell has 2n chromosomes in both G1 and G2. After replication, each chromosome simply has two sister chromatids joined at the centromere, so the count does not change.\nStatement II is correct. DNA replication in S phase raises the DNA content from 2C to 4C.\nSo Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which statement correctly describes cells in the G$_0$ stage?","image":"","options":["They are metabolically inactive and can never divide again","They are metabolically active but do not proliferate unless the organism requires it","They are actively replicating their DNA in preparation for mitosis","They are cells that have completed meiosis and are waiting for fertilisation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: cells that stop dividing leave $G_1$ and enter the quiescent stage ($G_0$).\n$G_0$ cells stay metabolically active but do not proliferate. They can divide again if the organism needs it, so (2) fits.\n(1) is the tempting trap. 'Inactive' here refers only to division, not to metabolism, and the cells are not permanently unable to divide.\n(3) describes S phase, and (4) is unrelated to $G_0$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Substage)\nA. Leptotene\nB. Zygotene\nC. Pachytene\nD. Diplotene\nList-II (Event)\nI. Dissolution of synaptonemal complex\nII. Appearance of recombination nodules\nIII. Chromosomes gradually become visible under light microscope\nIV. Synapsis of homologous chromosomes\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Leptotene: chromosomes gradually become visible under the light microscope → III.\nB. Zygotene: homologous chromosomes undergo synapsis → IV.\nC. Pachytene: recombination nodules appear, marking the sites of crossing over → II.\nD. Diplotene: the synaptonemal complex dissolves → I.\nSo the correct matching is A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A student is preparing a karyotype and needs to study the shape and structure of individual chromosomes clearly under a microscope. Which stage of mitosis should she select?","image":"","options":["Prophase","Metaphase","Anaphase","Telophase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): Metaphase is the stage at which chromosome morphology is most easily studied. Condensation is complete, and the chromosomes are clearly visible and lined up at the metaphase plate.\nThis makes metaphase ideal for karyotyping, so (2) fits.\n(1) is tempting but fails: in prophase, condensation has only just begun.\nIn anaphase the chromatids are moving apart.\nIn telophase the chromosomes decondense.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In animal cells, the centriole duplicates in the cytoplasm during:","image":"","options":["G1 phase","Prophase","G2 phase","S phase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key NCERT fact: during S phase, DNA replicates in the nucleus. In animal cells, the centriole also duplicates in the cytoplasm at the same time.\nThe tempting option is prophase. That is when the already duplicated centrosomes move towards opposite poles, not when they duplicate.\nG1 and G2 are gap phases. Centriole duplication is not linked to them in NCERT.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the quiescent stage (G0) is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Cells enter G0 by exiting the G2 phase of the cell cycle","Cells in G0 remain metabolically active","Cells in G0 do not proliferate unless the organism requires them to","Heart cells of adult animals are an example of cells that do not appear to divide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: cells that stop dividing leave the cycle from the G1 phase, not G2, and enter the quiescent G0 stage.\n(2) Correct: G0 cells stay metabolically active.\n(3) Correct: G0 cells divide only when the organism needs them to, for example in response to injury.\n(4) Correct: NCERT gives adult heart cells as an example of cells that do not appear to divide.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In animals, mitotic division occurs in diploid somatic cells, and there is no case in which haploid animal cells divide by mitosis.\nStatement II: Plants can show mitotic divisions in both haploid and diploid cells.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Male honey bees (drones) are haploid animals whose cells divide by mitosis, so the claim 'there is no case' is wrong.\nStatement II is correct. Plants show mitosis in both haploid cells (e.g., the gametophyte) and diploid cells (e.g., the sporophyte).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (sub-stage of prophase I) with List-II (characteristic event).\nList-I\nA. Zygotene\nB. Pachytene\nC. Diplotene\nD. Diakinesis\nList-II\ni. Terminalisation of chiasmata\nii. Dissolution of synaptonemal complex\niii. Synapsis of homologous chromosomes\niv. Appearance of recombination nodules\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Zygotene: homologous chromosomes pair up (synapsis), which is iii.\nB. Pachytene: recombination nodules appear at the sites of crossing over, which is iv.\nC. Diplotene: the synaptonemal complex dissolves and chiasmata become visible, which is ii.\nD. Diakinesis: chiasmata terminalise, which is i.\nThe correct match is A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A meiocyte of a plant with 2n = 16 has a DNA content of 2C in G1 phase. It completes meiosis normally. Which option correctly gives the chromosome number and DNA content per cell at the end of telophase I (each cell of the dyad) and at the end of telophase II (each cell of the tetrad)?","image":"","options":["Telophase I: 16 chromosomes, 2C; Telophase II: 8 chromosomes, C","Telophase I: 8 chromosomes, C; Telophase II: 8 chromosomes, C/2","Telophase I: 8 chromosomes, 2C; Telophase II: 8 chromosomes, C","Telophase I: 8 chromosomes, 4C; Telophase II: 8 chromosomes, 2C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $2n = 16$, and the G1 meiocyte has 2C DNA. S phase doubles the DNA to 4C, but the cell still has 16 chromosomes.\nAnaphase I separates homologues. Each dyad cell gets 8 chromosomes, and each chromosome still has two chromatids, so DNA = 2C.\nThere is no S phase in interkinesis. Anaphase II separates the sister chromatids, so each tetrad cell has 8 chromosomes and C DNA.\nOption (2) halves the DNA too early, and (4) ignores both divisions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Mitosis in haploid cells is common in plants but rare in animals. Which of the following best explains this difference?","image":"","options":["Plant cells lack centrioles, so only haploid plant cells can form a spindle","Animals do not carry out meiosis, so they never produce haploid cells","In plants, meiosis is followed by fusion of gametes, so their gametes must divide mitotically before fertilisation","In plants with alternation of generations, haploid spores divide mitotically to build a multicellular haploid gametophyte, whereas in most animals the only haploid cells are gametes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Plants show alternation of generations. Haploid spores formed by meiosis divide mitotically to build a multicellular haploid gametophyte, so haploid mitosis is common in plants.\nIn most animals, meiosis directly produces gametes, which are the only haploid cells. Haploid mitosis is therefore rare, e.g., in drones.\n(1) is wrong because spindle formation does not depend on ploidy.\n(2) is wrong because animals do carry out meiosis.\n(3) is wrong because it misstates the plant life cycle.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Mitosis is characterised by","image":"","options":["Reduction division","Equal division","Both reduction and equal division","Pairing of homologous chromosomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mitosis is called equational division. Each daughter cell gets the same number and kind of chromosomes as the parent cell ($2n \\\\to 2n$).\nOption (1) is wrong because reduction division (halving to $n$) is a feature of meiosis I.\nOption (4) is wrong because synapsis of homologues happens only in prophase I of meiosis.\nOption (3) is therefore also incorrect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 10 (MCQ Q5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In a human cell in culture with a cell cycle of about 24 hours, interphase occupies approximately:","image":"","options":["About 50% of the cycle","About 1 hour of the cycle","More than 95% of the cycle","About 75% of the cycle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: a human cell in culture divides about once every 24 hours.\nOf this, cell division proper (M phase) takes only about 1 hour.\nSo interphase takes about 23 of the 24 hours, which NCERT describes as more than 95% of the cycle.\nThe 1-hour figure in option (2) is the duration of M phase, not interphase.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Once sister chromatids separate at anaphase of mitosis, each chromatid is referred to as a:","image":"","options":["bivalent","daughter chromosome","dyad","chiasma"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"When the centromere splits at anaphase, each sister chromatid becomes an independent chromosome. NCERT calls it a daughter chromosome of the future daughter nucleus.\nThe tempting wrong option is 'dyad', but a dyad is a chromosome with two chromatids still joined, as in meiosis I.\nBivalent (paired homologues) and chiasma (crossing-over site) are also meiotic terms.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Onion root tip cells have 16 chromosomes, and each cell has a DNA content of 2C just after M phase. How many of the following statements about a dividing onion root tip cell are correct?\n(a) At G1: 16 chromosomes, DNA content 2C\n(b) After S phase: 32 chromosomes, DNA content 4C\n(c) At G2: 16 chromosomes, DNA content 4C\n(d) At metaphase: 32 chromatids in total\n(e) Each daughter cell after cytokinesis: 16 chromosomes, DNA content 2C","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: G1 follows M phase, so the cell has 16 chromosomes and 2C DNA.\n(b) Incorrect: S phase doubles DNA to 4C, but the chromosome number stays 16.\n(c) Correct: G2 has 16 chromosomes, each with two chromatids, and 4C DNA.\n(d) Correct: 16 chromosomes × 2 chromatids = 32 chromatids at metaphase.\n(e) Correct: mitosis restores 16 chromosomes and 2C DNA in each daughter cell.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A slide shows a dividing cell in which two groups of chromosomes are moving towards opposite poles, with the centromeres leading. Each moving chromosome clearly has two chromatids joined at its centromere, and each pole is receiving half the chromosome number of the parent cell. The cell is most likely in:","image":"","options":["Anaphase of mitosis","Anaphase I of meiosis","Anaphase II of meiosis","Metaphase I of meiosis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Two clues identify the stage: each moving chromosome still has two chromatids, and each pole receives half the parental chromosome number. This means homologues are separating while the centromeres stay intact, which happens in anaphase I.\nMitotic anaphase and anaphase II fail because the centromeres split, so each moving chromosome is a single chromatid.\nMetaphase I fails because the chromosomes are still aligned at the equator and are not yet moving to the poles.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which of the following cells of an adult animal does not appear to show cell division?","image":"","options":["Cells lining the gut","Cells of the upper layer of the epidermis","Precursor cells that replace blood cells","Heart cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key NCERT fact: some cells in adult animals do not appear to divide, and heart cells are the example given.\nNCERT also says that cells of the upper epidermis, gut lining and blood cells are constantly replaced by mitosis. That rules out options (1), (2) and (3).\nOption (3) refers to the precursor cells that keep producing new blood cells, so they clearly divide.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the onset of anaphase in mitosis?","image":"","options":["The centromeres of all chromosomes at the metaphase plate split simultaneously.","Centromeres split one after another, starting with the chromosomes nearest a pole.","Homologous chromosomes separate while their sister chromatids stay joined.","The nuclear envelope completely disintegrates."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): anaphase begins when the centromeres of all chromosomes at the metaphase plate split at the same time. The sister chromatids then move towards opposite poles.\nOption (2) is wrong because the splitting is simultaneous, not one chromosome after another.\nOption (3) describes anaphase I of meiosis, not mitosis.\nOption (4) is wrong because complete breakdown of the nuclear envelope marks the start of metaphase.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: At telophase of mitosis, individual chromosomes can no longer be distinguished, and the chromatin collects as a mass at each pole.\nStatement II: Chromosome condensation reaches its maximum during telophase.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: in telophase, chromosomes cluster at the poles and decondense. They lose their individuality and collect as a chromatin mass.\nStatement II is incorrect: condensation is complete (maximum) at metaphase. In telophase the chromosomes actually decondense.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A cell cannot keep growing indefinitely and must divide after attaining a certain size.\nReason (R): Growth of a cell disturbs the ratio between its nucleus and cytoplasm, which is restored by cell division.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: a cell stops growing at a certain size and then divides.\nR is true: growth increases the cytoplasm relative to the nucleus, disturbing the nucleo-cytoplasmic ratio.\nCell division restores that ratio. This is exactly why the cell must divide, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (stage of meiosis) with List-II (characteristic event):\nList-I\nA. Metaphase I\nB. Anaphase I\nC. Telophase I\nD. Anaphase II\nList-II\nI. Centromere of each chromosome splits and sister chromatids move to opposite poles\nII. Bivalents align on the equatorial plate\nIII. Homologous chromosomes separate with sister chromatids still attached at the centromere\nIV. Nuclear membrane and nucleolus reappear, and cytokinesis forms a dyad of cells","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Metaphase I: bivalents align on the equatorial plate, so A–II.\nB. Anaphase I: homologues separate, with sister chromatids still joined at the centromere, so B–III.\nC. Telophase I: the nuclear membrane and nucleolus reappear, and cytokinesis forms a dyad of cells, so C–IV.\nD. Anaphase II: centromeres split and sister chromatids move to opposite poles, so D–I.\nCommon mistake: swapping the anaphase I and anaphase II events.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (sub-stage of prophase I) with List-II (characteristic event).\nList-I:\nA. Leptotene\nB. Zygotene\nC. Pachytene\nD. Diplotene\nList-II:\nI. Appearance of recombination nodules\nII. Dissolution of synaptonemal complex\nIII. Chromosomes gradually become visible under the light microscope\nIV. Synapsis of homologous chromosomes","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A (Leptotene) – III: chromosomes compact and gradually become visible under the light microscope.\nB (Zygotene) – IV: homologous chromosomes pair (synapsis) and the synaptonemal complex forms.\nC (Pachytene) – I: recombination nodules appear, marking the sites of crossing over.\nD (Diplotene) – II: the synaptonemal complex dissolves and chiasmata become visible.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Mitosis is also called equational division because:","image":"","options":["The chromosomes line up at the equator of the cell during metaphase","The parent cell and the daughter cells have the same number of chromosomes","The cytoplasm is always divided into two exactly equal halves","Homologous chromosomes pair and exchange equal segments"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: Mitosis is called equational division because the daughter cells have the same chromosome number as the parent cell.\n(2) states exactly this, so it is the reason for the name.\n(1) is tempting but fails. Chromosomes do align at the equator during metaphase, but this is not why mitosis is called equational.\n(3) fails because cytokinesis need not give exactly equal halves.\n(4) describes synapsis and crossing over in meiosis, not mitosis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Terminalisation of chiasmata is the characteristic event of which substage of prophase I?","image":"","options":["Diakinesis","Diplotene","Pachytene","Zygotene"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Diakinesis is the last substage of prophase I. Its defining feature is terminalisation of chiasmata, in which the chiasmata move toward the ends of the chromosomes.\nDiplotene is the tempting wrong option. Chiasmata first become visible in diplotene, but they do not terminalise until diakinesis.\nPachytene is the stage of crossing over, and zygotene is the stage of synapsis.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): During mitotic anaphase, the centromere of each moving chromosome is at the leading edge, with the arms trailing behind.\nReason (R): Spindle fibres attach to chromosomes at kinetochores, which are disc-shaped structures on the surface of the centromere.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: in anaphase each chromosome moves centromere-first towards the pole, with its arms trailing.\nR is true: spindle fibres attach at kinetochores, which are small disc-shaped structures on the surface of the centromere.\nR explains A: the pulling force acts only at the kinetochore on the centromere. So the centromere leads and the arms follow passively.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"During mitotic anaphase, a daughter chromosome moving towards its pole is oriented such that:","image":"","options":["its arms lead and the centromere trails behind","its centromere is at the leading edge and the arms trail behind","it lies parallel to the equatorial plate while moving","its telomeres point towards the pole while the centromere stays at the equator"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): in anaphase, spindle fibres pull each daughter chromosome by its centromere (via the kinetochore).\nSo the centromere moves first and faces the pole. The arms are dragged behind it, which gives the typical V or J shape.\nOption (1) reverses this. Option (4) is wrong because the centromere leaves the equator. Option (3) describes metaphase alignment, not movement.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Onion root tip cells have 16 chromosomes. Suppose a meiocyte of a plant with the same chromosome number (2n = 16) has a DNA content of 2C in G1. What are the chromosome number and DNA content of each cell at metaphase II?","image":"","options":["8 chromosomes, 2C","16 chromosomes, 4C","8 chromosomes, C","16 chromosomes, 2C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 2n = 16, and DNA content is 2C in G1.\nS phase: DNA doubles to 4C, but the chromosome number stays 16 (each now has two chromatids).\nAnaphase I: homologues separate, so each daughter cell gets 8 two-chromatid chromosomes, i.e. 2C.\nInterkinesis: no replication, so the values do not change.\nSo at metaphase II each cell has 8 chromosomes and 2C DNA. The DNA falls to C only after anaphase II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following processes are based on mitotic cell division?\n(i) Development of a zygote into a multicellular embryo\n(ii) Constant replacement of cells lining the gut\n(iii) Increase in girth of a dicot stem by lateral cambium\n(iv) Formation of microspores from a microspore mother cell\n(v) Crossing over between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Mitotic: the zygote grows into a multicellular embryo by repeated mitosis.\n(ii) Mitotic: gut-lining cells are constantly replaced by mitosis.\n(iii) Mitotic: cambial cells divide mitotically to increase girth.\n(iv) Not mitotic: a microspore mother cell divides by meiosis to form a tetrad of microspores.\n(v) Not mitotic: crossing over occurs in prophase I (pachytene) of meiosis.\nSo the count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Plants are able to grow continuously throughout their life mainly because of mitotic divisions in","image":"","options":["mature xylem and phloem cells","apical and lateral meristems (cambium)","sclerenchyma fibres","guard cells of the epidermis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In plants, mitosis in meristematic tissues gives continuous, lifelong growth.\nApical meristems increase length, and lateral meristems (cambium) increase girth.\nMature xylem and phloem cells, sclerenchyma fibres and guard cells are permanent, differentiated cells. They do not normally divide.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are key features of meiosis?\n(i) Two sequential cycles of nuclear and cell division\n(ii) Two rounds of DNA replication\n(iii) Pairing of homologous chromosomes\n(iv) Recombination between sister chromatids\n(v) Formation of four haploid cells\n(vi) Occurrence in specialised diploid cells","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: meiosis has two sequential divisions, meiosis I and meiosis II.\n(ii) Incorrect: DNA replicates only once, in the S phase before meiosis I.\n(iii) Correct: homologous chromosomes pair (synapsis) in prophase I.\n(iv) Incorrect: recombination occurs between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes.\n(v) Correct: one meiocyte gives four haploid cells.\n(vi) Correct: meiosis occurs in specialised diploid cells (meiocytes).\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (event) with List-II (stage of mitosis):\nList-I\n(A) Chromosomes lose their identity as discrete elements\n(B) Chromosome morphology is most easily studied\n(C) Chromatids move to opposite poles\n(D) Condensation of chromosomal material begins\nList-II\n(I) Prophase\n(II) Metaphase\n(III) Anaphase\n(IV) Telophase","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: Chromosomes lose their identity as discrete elements in telophase, when they decondense. So A matches IV.\nB: Morphology is studied most easily in metaphase, when condensation is complete. So B matches II.\nC: Chromatids move to opposite poles in anaphase. So C matches III.\nD: Condensation of chromosomal material begins in prophase. So D matches I.\nThis gives A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A meiocyte of a plant has 16 chromosomes and 2C DNA content in G1. It replicates its DNA and completes meiosis I. What are the chromosome number and DNA content of each cell of the resulting dyad?","image":"","options":["16 chromosomes, 2C","8 chromosomes, C","8 chromosomes, 2C","16 chromosomes, 4C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: G1 meiocyte has 16 chromosomes and 2C DNA.\nAfter the S phase there are still 16 chromosomes, but DNA doubles to 4C. Each chromosome now has two sister chromatids.\nMeiosis I separates homologues, not sister chromatids. Each dyad cell therefore gets 8 chromosomes, each still with two chromatids.\nSo each cell has 8 chromosomes and $4C/2 = 2C$ DNA.\nTrap: C is reached only after meiosis II separates the sister chromatids.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"An onion root cell has 16 chromosomes ($2n = 16$). A meiocyte of onion is observed at pachytene of prophase I. What are the number of bivalents and the total number of chromatids in this cell?","image":"","options":["16 bivalents and 32 chromatids","8 bivalents and 16 chromatids","16 bivalents and 64 chromatids","8 bivalents and 32 chromatids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $2n = 16$, so the meiocyte has 16 chromosomes, which form 8 homologous pairs.\nAt pachytene the homologues are fully synapsed. Each pair is one bivalent, so there are $16 \\div 2 = 8$ bivalents.\nDNA replicated in the S phase before meiosis, so each chromosome has 2 sister chromatids. Each bivalent is therefore a tetrad of 4 chromatids.\nTotal chromatids $= 8 \\times 4 = 32$ (that is, $16 \\times 2$).\nOption (2) forgets replication, and option (1) counts every chromosome as a bivalent.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Metaphase is the most suitable stage for studying the morphology of chromosomes.\nReason (R): By metaphase, condensation of chromosomes is complete and they can be observed clearly.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: metaphase is the stage used to study chromosome morphology.\nR is true: chromosome condensation is complete by metaphase, so chromosomes are seen clearly.\nThe clarity that comes from complete condensation is exactly why metaphase is chosen. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many cycles of nuclear division and how many cycles of DNA replication are involved in one complete meiosis?","image":"","options":["One nuclear division and one DNA replication","Two nuclear divisions and two DNA replications","One nuclear division and two DNA replications","Two nuclear divisions and one DNA replication"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: Meiosis has two sequential nuclear divisions, meiosis I and meiosis II.\nOnly one round of DNA replication occurs, in the S phase before meiosis I. There is no replication before meiosis II.\nOne replication followed by two divisions is exactly why the chromosome number is halved.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following events of prophase I in the correct chronological order:\nP. Terminalisation of chiasmata\nQ. Formation of synaptonemal complex\nR. Appearance of recombination nodules\nS. Dissolution of synaptonemal complex","image":"","options":["R, Q, S, P","Q, R, S, P","Q, S, R, P","Q, R, P, S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Q comes first: the synaptonemal complex forms in zygotene.\nR comes next: recombination nodules appear in pachytene, which is where crossing over occurs.\nS follows: the synaptonemal complex dissolves at the start of diplotene.\nP is last: chiasmata terminalise in diakinesis.\nThe order Q, R, S, P makes sense because crossing over must happen while the homologues are still synapsed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about interkinesis is correct?","image":"","options":["It is a long phase during which DNA replicates a second time","It is the stage between meiosis II and the next cell cycle","It is generally short-lived, and no DNA replication occurs during it","It is the stage in which homologous chromosomes undergo synapsis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: DNA does not replicate a second time, and interkinesis is not a long phase.\n(2) Incorrect: interkinesis lies between meiosis I and meiosis II, not after meiosis II.\n(3) Correct: interkinesis is generally short-lived, and no DNA replication occurs during it.\n(4) Incorrect: synapsis happens in zygotene of prophase I.\nThe lack of replication is why meiosis has two divisions but only one round of DNA replication.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Meiosis II resembles a normal mitosis.\nReason (R): DNA replicates during interkinesis, so each chromosome entering meiosis II has two sister chromatids.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: meiosis II resembles mitosis because sister chromatids separate at anaphase II.\nR is false: DNA does not replicate during interkinesis. The two sister chromatids of each chromosome were formed in the S phase before meiosis I.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A meiocyte has 2C DNA and 2n chromosomes at G1. Match each stage in List-I with the correct DNA content and chromosome number per cell in List-II.\nList-I\nA. Cell at metaphase I\nB. Each cell at metaphase II\nC. Each cell formed after telophase II\nD. Meiocyte at G1\nList-II\ni. 2C, n\nii. 4C, 2n\niii. 2C, 2n\niv. C, n\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"D. At G1 the meiocyte has 2C and 2n → iii.\nA. After S phase, DNA doubles but chromosome number does not. So the metaphase I cell is 4C, 2n → ii.\nB. Anaphase I separates homologues. Each metaphase II cell therefore has n chromosomes, each with two chromatids, giving 2C, n → i.\nC. Anaphase II separates sister chromatids, so each cell after telophase II is C, n → iv.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (sub-stages of prophase I) with List-II (characteristic events).\nList-I:\nA. Zygotene\nB. Pachytene\nC. Diplotene\nD. Diakinesis\nList-II:\nI. Terminalisation of chiasmata\nII. Synapsis of homologous chromosomes\nIII. Appearance of recombination nodules\nIV. Dissolution of the synaptonemal complex","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A. Zygotene: homologous chromosomes pair up (synapsis) → II.\nB. Pachytene: recombination nodules appear, marking the sites of crossing over → III.\nC. Diplotene: the synaptonemal complex dissolves and chiasmata become visible → IV.\nD. Diakinesis: chiasmata terminalise → I.\nThe match is A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: During the S phase, the amount of DNA per cell increases from 2C to 4C.\nStatement II: During the S phase, the chromosome number of a diploid cell increases from 2n to 4n.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: DNA replication in S phase doubles the DNA content from 2C to 4C.\nStatement II is incorrect: the chromosome number stays 2n. Each chromosome now has two sister chromatids joined at the centromere, so the number of chromosomes does not change.\nTrap: do not assume that chromosome number doubles along with DNA content.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Cells that stop dividing leave the cell cycle and enter an inactive stage. What is this stage called, and from which phase do the cells exit to enter it?","image":"","options":["Quiescent stage (G$_0$), entered from the G$_2$ phase","Interkinesis, entered from the S phase","Quiescent stage (G$_0$), entered from the G$_1$ phase","Interphase, entered from the M phase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: cells that do not divide further exit the G$_1$ phase and enter an inactive stage called the quiescent stage (G$_0$).\nCells in G$_0$ stay metabolically active but no longer proliferate unless called on to do so.\nThe tempting option (1) is wrong because cells exit before DNA replication, in G$_1$, not after it in G$_2$.\nInterkinesis is the stage between meiosis I and II, so option (2) does not fit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is described as a continuous process throughout the cell cycle?","image":"","options":["DNA synthesis","Cell growth in terms of increase in cytoplasm","Separation of daughter chromosomes","Division of cytoplasm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cell growth, meaning increase in cytoplasm, is a continuous process throughout the cell cycle.\nDNA synthesis is the tempting option, but it happens only in one specific stage, the S phase.\nSeparation of daughter chromosomes and division of the cytoplasm happen only in M phase.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A diploid animal cell is observed in interphase. Its nucleus has 4C DNA, it has two centrosomes in the cytoplasm, and it is actively synthesising proteins needed for mitosis. The cell is most likely in:","image":"","options":["G1 phase","G0 phase","Early S phase, just after DNA replication begins","G2 phase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A 4C DNA content means DNA replication is complete, so the cell has finished S phase.\nTwo centrosomes mean the centriole has already duplicated, which also happens in S phase.\nActive synthesis of proteins for mitosis is the hallmark of G2.\nG1 and G0 cells have only 2C DNA.\nA cell in early S phase has not yet reached 4C.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Small disc-shaped structures present on the surface of the centromeres, to which spindle fibres attach during metaphase, are called:","image":"","options":["Centrioles","Asters","Kinetochores","Chromomeres"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Kinetochores are small disc-shaped structures on the surface of the centromere. Spindle fibres attach to them, and they hold chromosomes at the equator during metaphase.\nAsters are microtubules radiating from the centrosome, so they are not attachment sites on the chromosome.\nCentrioles lie inside the centrosome, not on the chromosome.\nChromomeres are bead-like condensed regions along the chromatin, not spindle attachment points.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In the oocytes of some vertebrates, one substage of prophase I can last for months or even years. Which substage is it?","image":"","options":["Leptotene","Pachytene","Diakinesis","Diplotene"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that diplotene can last for months or years in the oocytes of some vertebrates.\nThe tempting option is pachytene. It is long compared with leptotene and zygotene, but it is not the substage described as lasting for years.\nLeptotene and diakinesis are relatively short substages.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (cell stage of a diploid meiocyte lineage) with List-II (chromosome number N and DNA content C per cell). The meiocyte has 2n chromosomes and 2C DNA in G1.\nList-I\n(A) Meiocyte in G1\n(B) Meiocyte in G2\n(C) Each cell after telophase I and cytokinesis\n(D) Each cell after telophase II and cytokinesis\nList-II\n(I) n, 1C\n(II) n, 2C\n(III) 2n, 4C\n(IV) 2n, 2C","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) G1 meiocyte: 2n chromosomes, 2C DNA, so A matches IV.\n(B) G2 meiocyte: replication gives 4C, while the number stays 2n, so B matches III.\n(C) After telophase I: homologues have separated, giving n chromosomes. Each still has two chromatids, so DNA is 2C and C matches II.\n(D) After telophase II: sister chromatids have separated, giving n and 1C, so D matches I.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Furrow\n(b) Cell plate\n(c) Syncytium\n(d) Karyokinesis\nList-II\n(i) Segregation of duplicated chromosomes into daughter nuclei\n(ii) Liquid endosperm of coconut\n(iii) Cytokinesis in animal cells\n(iv) Precursor of middle lamella","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Furrow – (iii). Animal cells divide their cytoplasm by a furrow in the plasma membrane.\n(b) Cell plate – (iv). The cell plate later represents the middle lamella between adjacent walls.\n(c) Syncytium – (ii). The liquid endosperm of coconut is multinucleate.\n(d) Karyokinesis – (i). It separates the duplicated chromosomes into two daughter nuclei.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (substage of prophase I) with List-II (characteristic event):\nList-I\nA. Leptotene\nB. Zygotene\nC. Diplotene\nD. Diakinesis\nList-II\n(i) Terminalisation of chiasmata\n(ii) Dissolution of the synaptonemal complex\n(iii) Chromosomes gradually become visible under the light microscope\n(iv) Formation of the synaptonemal complex during synapsis\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Leptotene → (iii): chromosomes compact and gradually become visible under the light microscope.\nB. Zygotene → (iv): homologues pair (synapsis) and the synaptonemal complex forms.\nC. Diplotene → (ii): the synaptonemal complex dissolves, and the homologues begin to separate except at the chiasmata.\nD. Diakinesis → (i): the chiasmata terminalise.\nDo not confuse chiasma appearance (diplotene) with terminalisation (diakinesis).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Bivalents are seen more clearly as tetrads in pachytene than in zygotene.\nReason (R): During pachytene, the four chromatids of each bivalent become distinct.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Bivalents form during zygotene, but they are seen clearly as tetrads only in pachytene.\nR is true. In pachytene, the four chromatids of each bivalent become distinct.\nR correctly explains A: a bivalent looks like a tetrad precisely because its four chromatids can now be told apart.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Mitosis is also called equational division because:","image":"","options":["The two daughter cells are always equal in size","The chromosome number in the parent cell and in the progeny cells is the same","The DNA content of each daughter cell equals that of the parent cell at G2","Equal amounts of cytoplasm and organelles go to both daughter cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT calls mitosis equational division because the chromosome number stays the same from parent to progeny cells.\nA diploid cell gives two diploid daughter cells, and a haploid cell gives two haploid daughter cells.\nOption (3) is the tempting one, but it is wrong. At G2 the parent has 4C DNA, while each daughter cell receives 2C.\nDaughter cells need not be equal in size or get exactly equal cytoplasm, so (1) and (4) are not the basis of the term.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Crossing over during prophase I is an enzyme-mediated process. The enzyme involved is:","image":"","options":["Ligase","Helicase","Recombinase","DNA polymerase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Crossing over is the exchange of genetic material between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes during pachytene.\nNCERT states that this process is mediated by the enzyme recombinase.\nLigase, helicase and DNA polymerase are tempting because they act on DNA. They are, however, the enzymes of DNA replication (joining fragments, unwinding and synthesis), not the ones that mediate recombination.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"An onion root tip cell has 16 chromosomes after M phase.\nStatement I: This cell will have 16 chromosomes at G1 and 32 chromosomes after S phase.\nStatement II: During S phase, the DNA content per cell doubles from 2C to 4C.\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect: S phase doubles the DNA but not the chromosome number. The cell still has 16 chromosomes after S phase, each now made of two sister chromatids.\nStatement II is correct: DNA content per cell rises from 2C to 4C during S phase.\nThe common error is to count chromatids (32) as chromosomes.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (event) with List-II (phase of cell cycle):\nList-I\n(a) Duplication of centrosome/centriole\n(b) Initiation of condensation of chromosomal material\n(c) Completion of chromosome condensation\n(d) Synthesis of proteins in preparation for mitosis\nList-II\n(i) Prophase\n(ii) G2 phase\n(iii) S phase\n(iv) Metaphase","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) The centrosome (centriole) duplicates in the cytoplasm during S phase, so (a)-(iii).\n(b) Chromosomal material starts to condense in prophase, so (b)-(i).\n(c) Condensation is complete by metaphase, so (c)-(iv).\n(d) Proteins needed for mitosis are made in G2, so (d)-(ii).\nWatch the trap: prophase is when the duplicated centrosomes move apart, not when they duplicate.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A meiocyte of onion has 2n = 16. How many bivalents and how many chromatids will be present in this cell at pachytene?","image":"","options":["16 bivalents and 32 chromatids","8 bivalents and 32 chromatids","8 bivalents and 16 chromatids","16 bivalents and 64 chromatids"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $2n = 16$ chromosomes, each already replicated into two chromatids.\nBivalents: the homologous chromosomes pair, so the number of bivalents is $16/2 = 8$.\nChromatids: each bivalent is a tetrad with 4 chromatids, so the total is $8 \\times 4 = 32$.\nSo at pachytene the cell has 8 bivalents and 32 chromatids.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"The mitotic apparatus of an animal cell is formed by:","image":"","options":["Two centrioles and the nuclear envelope","Two asters together with spindle fibres","Kinetochores and the cell plate","Nucleolus and spindle fibres"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"During prophase in animal cells, the two centrosomes move to opposite poles.\nEach centrosome radiates microtubules called asters.\nThe two asters together with the spindle fibres form the mitotic apparatus.\nThe nuclear envelope disappears during mitosis, so it is not part of this apparatus.\nThe cell plate forms only in plant cells, during cytokinesis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Meiosis I begins before the parental chromosomes replicate, and replication occurs during interkinesis.\nStatement II: Identical sister chromatids are produced during the S phase that precedes meiosis I.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Meiosis I begins only after the parental chromosomes have replicated in the S phase. Interkinesis has no DNA replication.\nStatement II is correct. The S phase before meiosis I produces identical sister chromatids.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A primary oocyte of a vertebrate is observed in a state that can persist for months or even years. Its homologous chromosomes tend to separate from each other but remain joined at X-shaped points. The synaptonemal complex is absent and the nuclear envelope is still intact. Identify the substage and the event that will follow next.","image":"","options":["Diplotene; chiasmata will terminalise and the nuclear envelope will break down in diakinesis","Pachytene; recombination nodules will appear in diplotene","Diakinesis; the synaptonemal complex will dissolve in metaphase I","Zygotene; recombinase will bring about crossing over in diplotene"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The clues identify diplotene:\n- Homologues are separating but stay held at X-shaped chiasmata.\n- The synaptonemal complex has already dissolved.\n- In vertebrate oocytes this stage can last for months or years (dictyotene).\nThe next substage is diakinesis. In it, chiasmata terminalise, the spindle forms and the nuclear envelope breaks down.\nThe other options give the wrong substage or the wrong order of events.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (stage of meiosis) with List-II (event).\nList-I:\nA. Diakinesis\nB. Anaphase I\nC. Telophase I\nD. Anaphase II\nList-II:\nI. Homologous chromosomes separate while sister chromatids stay joined at centromeres\nII. Splitting of centromeres allows sister chromatids to move to opposite poles\nIII. Terminalisation of chiasmata\nIV. Formation of a dyad of cells","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A. Diakinesis: chiasmata terminalise, which is III.\nB. Anaphase I: homologous chromosomes separate, but sister chromatids stay joined at their centromeres, which is I.\nC. Telophase I: nuclear envelope reforms and cytokinesis follows, giving a dyad of cells, which is IV.\nD. Anaphase II: centromeres split, so sister chromatids move to opposite poles, which is II.\nOption (1) is tempting but wrong: it puts centromere splitting in anaphase I, and that happens only in anaphase II.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A single specialised diploid cell completes meiosis. What is formed at the end of meiosis II?","image":"","options":["Two diploid cells","Two haploid cells","Four haploid cells","Four diploid cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: One diploid meiocyte goes through two successive divisions.\nMeiosis I halves the chromosome number and gives two haploid cells (a dyad).\nMeiosis II is equational, so each of these two cells divides again. The result is four haploid cells (a tetrad).\nOption (2) describes the stage after meiosis I only, not the end of meiosis II.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Plant cells do not divide their cytoplasm by forming a furrow in the plasma membrane.\nReason (R): Plant cells are enclosed by a relatively inextensible cell wall.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Plant cells do not divide their cytoplasm by furrowing the plasma membrane. Instead, a cell plate forms in the centre and grows outward.\nR is true. Plant cells are enclosed by a relatively inextensible cell wall.\nR explains A. A rigid wall cannot be pinched inward by a furrow, so plant cells must use the cell plate mechanism instead.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The bivalent formed during zygotene is more clearly visible in the following stage.\nStatement II: In pachytene, the four chromatids of each bivalent become distinct, and the bivalent clearly appears as a tetrad.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Bivalents form during zygotene, but they are seen more clearly in the next stage, pachytene.\nStatement II is correct. In pachytene the four chromatids of each bivalent become distinct, so the bivalent clearly appears as a tetrad.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Meiosis II is not regarded as the reduction step of meiosis, even though DNA content per cell is halved during it.\nReason (R): During meiosis II, sister chromatids separate, so each daughter cell keeps the same chromosome number (n) as the cell that entered meiosis II.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Meiosis II takes a cell with $n$ chromosomes, each having two chromatids (2C DNA), and produces cells with $n$ chromosomes of one chromatid each (1C DNA). DNA is halved, but chromosome number is not.\nR is true. Only sister chromatids separate in meiosis II, so the chromosome number stays $n$.\n'Reduction' means halving the chromosome number. That happens in meiosis I, when homologues separate. R explains why meiosis II is not the reduction step.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Human cells growing in culture are used to illustrate a typical eukaryotic cell cycle. Approximately how often do such cells divide?","image":"","options":["Once every 90 minutes","Once every 24 hours","Once every 12 hours","Once every 48 hours"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT uses human cells in culture as a typical eukaryotic cell cycle.\nThese cells divide about once every 24 hours.\nThe tempting option, 90 minutes, is the cycle time of yeast, not human cells.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"At the end of telophase I, the nuclear membrane and nucleolus reappear and cytokinesis follows. What is the resulting pair of cells called?","image":"","options":["Tetrad of cells","Bivalent","Syncytium","Dyad of cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: telophase I is followed by cytokinesis, which produces two cells. NCERT calls this a dyad of cells.\nOption (1) is tempting, but a tetrad of cells (four haploid cells) forms only after meiosis II.\nA bivalent is a pair of synapsed homologous chromosomes, not a pair of cells.\nA syncytium is a multinucleate cell formed when nuclear division is not followed by cytokinesis.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Heart cells of an adult mammal\n(B) Body cells of a drone (male honey bee)\n(C) Cells of a fern prothallus\n(D) Cells lining the gut\nList-II\n(I) Haploid plant cells that divide by mitosis\n(II) Cells that are constantly replaced through mitosis\n(III) Cells that do not appear to divide and stay out of the active cycle\n(IV) Haploid animal cells that divide by mitosis","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Adult heart cells do not appear to divide and stay out of the active cycle → III.\n(B) Drone body cells are haploid animal cells that divide by mitosis → IV.\n(C) The fern prothallus is a haploid gametophyte that grows by mitosis → I.\n(D) Gut lining cells are constantly replaced through mitosis → II.\nThe main trap is swapping the haploid plant (C) and haploid animal (B) examples.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"During prophase I of meiosis, recombination nodules appear. These are the sites where crossing over occurs between non-sister chromatids. In which substage do they appear?","image":"","options":["Zygotene","Pachytene","Diplotene","Leptotene"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key NCERT fact: pachytene is the stage when recombination nodules appear on the synapsed bivalents.\nAt these nodules the enzyme recombinase brings about crossing over, an exchange of segments between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes.\nZygotene is tempting, but it is the stage of synapsis and synaptonemal complex formation, not crossing over.\nDiplotene only shows chiasmata, the X-shaped sites left after crossing over is complete.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A culture of human cells is dividing asynchronously, so cells are randomly distributed across the cell cycle. Each cell has a 24-hour cycle in which M phase lasts about 1 hour. If you count cells in a stained sample, roughly what percentage would you expect to find in M phase?","image":"","options":["About 50%","About 25%","About 4%","About 95%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: the cycle lasts 24 h and M phase lasts about 1 h. Cells are dividing asynchronously, so they are spread randomly across the cycle.\nFormula: % of cells in a phase = (duration of phase ÷ total cycle time) × 100\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{24} \\times 100 \\approx 4.2\\%$\nSo about 4% of cells should be in M phase. The value 95% is the share of interphase, which is the common trap.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In which phase of the cell cycle does the amount of DNA per cell double?","image":"","options":["G1 phase","G2 phase","S phase","M phase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"DNA replication occurs only in the S (synthesis) phase, so the DNA content per cell doubles from 2C to 4C there.\nThe chromosome number does not change in S phase.\nG1 and G2 are gap phases in which the cell grows but does not replicate DNA.\nIn M phase, the already doubled DNA is shared between the daughter cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A diploid cell with 2n chromosomes and DNA content 2C passes through the S phase. What are its chromosome number and DNA content at the end of S phase?","image":"","options":["4n and 4C","2n and 2C","2n and 4C","4n and 2C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In S phase, DNA replication doubles the DNA content from 2C to 4C.\nThe chromosome number stays 2n, because each chromosome now has two sister chromatids held at a single centromere.\nThe common trap is option (1). Chromosomes are counted by centromeres, so the number does not double with the DNA.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Identify the wrong statement about meiosis","image":"","options":["Pairing of homologous chromosomes","Four haploid cells are formed","At the end of meiosis the number of chromosomes are reduced to half","Two cycle of DNA replication occurs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) True: homologous chromosomes pair (synapsis) in prophase I.\n(2) True: one meiotic cycle gives four haploid cells.\n(3) True: the chromosome number is halved, so meiosis is reduction division.\n(4) Wrong: meiosis has only one round of DNA replication (S phase), followed by two successive divisions. This is exactly why the chromosome number is halved.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 10 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding the significance of mitosis is correct?","image":"","options":["Mitosis always produces diploid daughter cells, irrespective of the ploidy of the parent cell","Heart cells of adult animals divide continuously by mitosis to repair the heart","Mitosis in the meristems allows plants to grow throughout their life, whereas some cells of adult animals, like heart cells, do not appear to divide","Restoration of the nucleo-cytoplasmic ratio requires the chromosome number to be halved during division"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: mitosis keeps the parent's ploidy. A haploid cell gives haploid daughters.\n(2) Incorrect: adult heart cells do not appear to divide. They stay in the inactive G0 stage.\n(3) Correct: apical and lateral meristems divide by mitosis all through life, so plants grow continuously. Some adult animal cells, such as heart cells, do not divide.\n(4) Incorrect: mitosis restores the nucleo-cytoplasmic ratio by producing two cells. It never halves the chromosome number.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following gives the correct order of the substages of prophase I of meiosis?","image":"","options":["Leptotene → Pachytene → Zygotene → Diplotene → Diakinesis","Zygotene → Leptotene → Pachytene → Diakinesis → Diplotene","Leptotene → Zygotene → Diplotene → Pachytene → Diakinesis","Leptotene → Zygotene → Pachytene → Diplotene → Diakinesis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Prophase I has five substages, and each has a key event:\n1. Leptotene: chromosomes condense.\n2. Zygotene: homologues pair (synapsis).\n3. Pachytene: crossing over occurs.\n4. Diplotene: the synaptonemal complex dissolves and chiasmata appear.\n5. Diakinesis: chiasmata terminalise and the cell moves into metaphase I.\nOnly option (4) lists them in this order. Option (3) wrongly places diplotene before pachytene.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In animals, mitosis normally occurs only in diploid somatic cells. Which of the following is the NCERT example of an animal in which haploid cells divide by mitosis?","image":"","options":["Male honey bee","Female worker honey bee","Human male","Earthworm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: in animals, mitosis is usually seen only in diploid somatic cells. The exception is the male honey bee (drone).\nDrones develop from unfertilised eggs, so they are haploid, and their body cells still divide by mitosis.\nWorker bees are the tempting wrong choice. They develop from fertilised eggs, so they are diploid.\nHuman males and earthworms are diploid, so they fit the general rule.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): At the start of metaphase, the chromosomes are spread throughout the cytoplasm of the cell.\nReason (R): The complete disintegration of the nuclear envelope marks the beginning of metaphase.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: according to NCERT, the complete disintegration of the nuclear envelope marks the start of metaphase.\nA is true: once the nuclear envelope is gone, the chromosomes are spread through the cytoplasm of the cell.\nR explains A, because it is the loss of the nuclear boundary that leaves the chromosomes free in the cytoplasm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A diploid (2n) cell has a DNA content of 2C immediately after M phase. It then exits the cycle and enters the quiescent stage. What are the DNA content and chromosome number of this cell while in G$_0$?","image":"","options":["4C and 2n","2C and 2n","4C and 4n","2C and n"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: after M phase the cell is 2n with 2C DNA.\nCells enter $G_0$ by exiting from $G_1$, which comes before S phase. So DNA replication has not occurred.\nThe cell therefore keeps 2C DNA. Chromosome number changes only at division, so it stays 2n.\n4C would be the content only after S phase, which this cell never reaches.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the cell cycle are correct?\n(a) DNA synthesis takes place throughout interphase.\n(b) Cell growth, DNA replication and cell division must be coordinated so that progeny cells receive intact genomes.\n(c) In a typical 24-hour human cell cycle, cell division proper lasts only about one hour.\n(d) The sequence of events of the cell cycle includes duplication of the genome and synthesis of other cell constituents.\n(e) Interphase occupies less than half the duration of the cell cycle.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Incorrect: DNA synthesis is confined to the S phase. It does not occur throughout interphase.\n(b) Correct: growth, DNA replication and division must be coordinated so that each daughter cell gets an intact genome.\n(c) Correct: in a 24-hour human cell cycle, M phase lasts only about 1 hour.\n(d) Correct: the cycle includes duplication of the genome and synthesis of other cell constituents.\n(e) Incorrect: interphase takes up more than 95% of the cycle, not less than half.\nSo three statements, (b), (c) and (d), are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): X-shaped chiasmata become visible during diplotene.\nReason (R): At the beginning of diplotene, the synaptonemal complex dissolves and the recombined homologous chromosomes tend to separate from each other except at the sites of crossovers.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. X-shaped chiasmata are the characteristic feature of diplotene.\nR is true. At the start of diplotene the synaptonemal complex dissolves, and the homologues move apart everywhere except at the crossover points.\nR explains A. Because the homologues stay joined only at the crossover sites, these points appear as X-shaped chiasmata.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: At the time of cytoplasmic division, organelles such as mitochondria and plastids get distributed between the two daughter cells.\nStatement II: In animal cells, cytokinesis begins at the centre of the cell and spreads outward towards the plasma membrane.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. During cytoplasmic division (cytokinesis), organelles such as mitochondria and plastids are distributed between the two daughter cells.\nStatement II is incorrect. In animal cells, a furrow appears in the plasma membrane and deepens inward until it joins in the centre. Growth from the centre outward describes the cell plate of plant cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following structures are NOT seen when a cell at the end of mitotic prophase is viewed under the microscope?\nGolgi complex, endoplasmic reticulum, nucleolus, nuclear envelope, mitochondria, plasma membrane","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that cells at the end of prophase do not show the Golgi complex, endoplasmic reticulum, nucleolus or nuclear envelope.\nThat makes 4 structures that are not seen.\nMitochondria remain visible and are later shared between the daughter cells.\nThe plasma membrane stays intact throughout mitosis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Meiosis involves how many cycles of nuclear and cell division, and how many cycles of DNA replication?","image":"","options":["Two cycles of division and two cycles of DNA replication","One cycle of division and one cycle of DNA replication","Two cycles of division and only one cycle of DNA replication","One cycle of division and two cycles of DNA replication"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: meiosis involves two sequential cycles of nuclear and cell division (meiosis I and meiosis II) but only one cycle of DNA replication.\nReplication happens in the S phase before meiosis I. Interkinesis has no S phase.\nThis single replication followed by two divisions is why the chromosome number is halved.\nThe options with two replications are wrong, and so are the options with only one division.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In plant cells, cytokinesis takes place by forming a cell plate in the centre of the cell, which grows outward to meet the lateral walls.\nReason (R): Plant cells are enclosed by a relatively inextensible cell wall, so they cannot divide by furrowing of the plasma membrane.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: in plant cells, wall formation starts in the centre as a cell plate. The cell plate grows outward to meet the existing lateral walls and becomes the middle lamella.\nR is true: plant cells have a relatively inextensible cell wall, so a furrow cannot form as it does in animal cells.\nR correctly explains A: the rigid wall is exactly why plants need the cell-plate mechanism.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"At which stage of mitosis is the morphology of chromosomes most easily studied?","image":"","options":["Prophase","Metaphase","Anaphase","Telophase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chromosome condensation begins in prophase and is complete by metaphase.\nFully condensed metaphase chromosomes, each with two sister chromatids held at the centromere, are the clearest to observe. This is why chromosome morphology is studied at metaphase.\nIn prophase, condensation is still in progress.\nIn anaphase, the chromatids are moving apart.\nIn telophase, chromosomes decondense and lose their individuality.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A meiocyte of a plant has 2n = 20 chromosomes, and its DNA content at G1 is 2C. What will be the chromosome number and DNA content of each cell of the dyad formed at the end of telophase I (after cytokinesis)?","image":"","options":["10 chromosomes; 1C DNA","10 chromosomes; 2C DNA","20 chromosomes; 2C DNA","20 chromosomes; 4C DNA"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: meiocyte 2n = 20, DNA at G1 = 2C.\nAfter S phase the DNA doubles to 4C. The chromosome number stays 20, and each chromosome now has two sister chromatids.\nAnaphase I: homologous chromosomes separate, but the sister chromatids stay joined at the centromere. Each pole therefore receives 10 chromosomes, each with two chromatids.\nSo each dyad cell has 10 chromosomes and $4C/2 = 2C$ DNA.\nTrap (1): DNA does not fall to 1C yet. That happens only when the sister chromatids separate in anaphase II.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. G1 phase\nB. S phase\nC. G2 phase\nD. G0 phase\nList-II\ni. Inactive stage; cells are metabolically active but do not proliferate\nii. Amount of DNA per cell doubles\niii. Proteins are synthesised in preparation for mitosis\niv. Cell grows continuously but does not replicate its DNA","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–iv: In G1, the cell is metabolically active and grows continuously, but its DNA does not replicate.\nB–ii: In S phase, DNA replicates, so the amount of DNA per cell doubles.\nC–iii: In G2, proteins are synthesised in preparation for mitosis.\nD–i: G0 is the quiescent stage. The cell stays metabolically active but does not proliferate.\nOnly option (1) gives all four of these pairs.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the eukaryotic cell cycle are correct?\n(i) In the average 24-hour human cell cycle, interphase occupies more than 95% of the time.\n(ii) Cell growth, in terms of increase in cytoplasm, occurs only during the G1 phase.\n(iii) DNA synthesis is restricted to one specific stage of the cell cycle.\n(iv) All cell types of a given organism take the same time to complete one cell cycle.\n(v) The events that distribute replicated chromosomes to daughter nuclei are under genetic control.\n(vi) Karyokinesis is always followed by cytokinesis.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: interphase takes up more than 95% of the cycle.\n(ii) Incorrect: growth in cytoplasm is continuous, not limited to G1.\n(iii) Correct: DNA synthesis occurs only in the S phase.\n(iv) Incorrect: cycle duration differs between cell types.\n(v) Correct: the distribution of replicated chromosomes to daughter nuclei is genetically controlled.\n(vi) Incorrect: in coconut liquid endosperm, karyokinesis occurs without cytokinesis, forming a syncytium.\nSo three statements, (i), (iii) and (v), are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: During S phase, the DNA content of a diploid cell increases from 2C to 4C, but its chromosome number remains 2n.\nStatement II: In animal cells, both DNA replication and centriole duplication take place inside the nucleus during S phase.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In S phase the DNA doubles from 2C to 4C, but each chromosome now has two sister chromatids, so the number stays 2n.\nStatement II is incorrect. DNA replicates in the nucleus, but in animal cells the centriole duplicates in the cytoplasm, not inside the nucleus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Human cells growing in culture complete one cell cycle in about 24 hours. Which organism does NCERT cite as being able to finish a cell cycle in only about 90 minutes?","image":"","options":["Onion root tip cells","Yeast","Male honey bee","Human heart cells"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that cell cycle duration varies between organisms. Yeast can complete a cell cycle in about 90 minutes, while human cells in culture take about 24 hours.\nHeart cells are a trap: adult heart cells do not divide and stay in the G0 stage.\nOnion root tip is the standard material for observing mitosis, not the example of a 90-minute cycle.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Select the correct statement about G1 phase","image":"","options":["Cell is metabolically inactive","DNA in the cell does not replicate","It is not a phase of synthesis of macromolecules","Cell stops growing"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"G$_1$ phase is the interval between mitosis and the start of DNA replication.\nIn G$_1$ the cell is metabolically active. It makes RNA and proteins and keeps growing, so options (1), (3) and (4) are wrong.\nDNA does not replicate in G$_1$. Replication happens only in the S phase, so (2) is correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 10 (MCQ Q10)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A diploid cell sampled midway through S phase can have a DNA content between 2C and 4C while its chromosome number is still 2n.\nReason (R): In S phase, DNA is replicated progressively and its amount doubles, but there is no increase in chromosome number.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Replication continues throughout S phase, so a cell sampled midway has a DNA amount between 2C and 4C. Its chromosome number is still 2n.\nR is true. NCERT states that DNA doubles in S phase with no increase in chromosome number.\nR explains A: gradual replication gives the intermediate DNA amount, and replication does not add chromosomes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (stage) with List-II (event):\nList-I\n(A) Metaphase I\n(B) Anaphase I\n(C) Metaphase II\n(D) Telophase II\nList-II\n(I) Microtubules attach to kinetochores of sister chromatids at the equator\n(II) Bivalents align on the equatorial plate\n(III) Four haploid daughter cells are formed after cytokinesis\n(IV) Homologues separate while sister chromatids remain joined at centromeres","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Metaphase I: homologous pairs (bivalents) line up on the equatorial plate, so A–II.\n(B) Anaphase I: homologues separate, but sister chromatids stay joined at the centromere, so B–IV.\n(C) Metaphase II: single chromosomes align, and microtubules from opposite poles attach to the kinetochores of sister chromatids, so C–I.\n(D) Telophase II: cytokinesis gives a tetrad of four haploid cells, so D–III.\nTrap: bivalents align only in metaphase I. In metaphase II single chromosomes align.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A meiocyte of a plant has 2n = 20 chromosomes, and its DNA content at G1 is 2C. What are the number of chromosomes and the DNA content in each cell at metaphase II?","image":"","options":["20 chromosomes, 4C","10 chromosomes, 1C","20 chromosomes, 2C","10 chromosomes, 2C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $2n = 20$, DNA at G1 = 2C.\nAfter S phase the cell still has 20 chromosomes, but DNA rises to 4C because each chromosome now has two chromatids.\nAnaphase I separates homologues, so each daughter cell gets 10 chromosomes. Each is still two chromatids, so DNA = 2C.\nNo DNA replication occurs in interkinesis, so at metaphase II each cell has 10 chromosomes and 2C DNA.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Cytokinesis in animal cells involves the appearance of a furrow in the plasma membrane.\n(ii) Cell plate formation begins at the lateral walls and proceeds towards the centre.\n(iii) Mitochondria and plastids are distributed between daughter cells during cytoplasmic division.\n(iv) A syncytium results when cytokinesis is not followed by karyokinesis.\n(v) The cell plate represents the middle lamella between the walls of two adjacent cells.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: animal cytokinesis proceeds by a furrow in the plasma membrane.\n(ii) Incorrect: the cell plate forms at the centre and grows outward to the lateral walls.\n(iii) Correct: mitochondria and plastids are shared between the daughter cells.\n(iv) Incorrect: a syncytium forms when karyokinesis is NOT followed by cytokinesis. The statement reverses the order.\n(v) Correct: the cell plate represents the middle lamella.\nThree statements, (i), (iii) and (v), are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"The liquid endosperm of coconut is multinucleate. This condition arises because:","image":"","options":["Cytokinesis occurs without karyokinesis","Karyokinesis is not followed by cytokinesis","Several cells fuse after normal mitosis","DNA replicates repeatedly without nuclear division"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"When nuclear division (karyokinesis) is not followed by cytoplasmic division (cytokinesis), many nuclei end up in one cell. This multinucleate condition is called a syncytium.\nThe liquid endosperm of coconut is the standard NCERT example.\nOption (3) is wrong: cells fusing after normal mitosis is not how this endosperm forms.\nOption (4) is wrong: replication without nuclear division would raise ploidy, not the number of nuclei.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Mitosis is also called equational division.\nReason (R): In mitosis the parent cell and the progeny cells have the same number of chromosomes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT calls mitosis equational division.\nR is true. Daughter cells receive the same chromosome number as the parent ($2n \\rightarrow 2n$, or $n \\rightarrow n$).\nR is the correct explanation of A. Mitosis is called 'equational' precisely because the chromosome number stays equal in parent and progeny.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following animals do haploid cells divide by mitosis, an exception to the general rule that animal mitosis occurs only in diploid somatic cells?","image":"","options":["Male honey bee","Human female","Frog tadpole","Earthworm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In animals, mitosis normally occurs only in diploid somatic cells.\nMale honey bees (drones) develop from unfertilised eggs, so they are haploid. Their haploid cells still divide by mitosis, which is the exception NCERT cites.\nHumans, frog tadpoles and earthworms all have diploid body cells, so their mitosis follows the general rule.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about meiosis II are correct?\n(i) It is preceded by DNA replication during interkinesis.\n(ii) Prophase II is much simpler than prophase I.\n(iii) The nuclear membrane disappears by the end of prophase II.\n(iv) In metaphase II, spindle microtubules from opposite poles attach to kinetochores of sister chromatids.\n(v) It is usually initiated before the chromosomes have fully elongated after telophase I.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: DNA does not replicate in interkinesis. The only replication of meiosis happens in the S phase before meiosis I.\n(ii) Correct: prophase II has no synapsis or crossing over, so it is much simpler than prophase I.\n(iii) Correct: the nuclear membrane disappears by the end of prophase II.\n(iv) Correct: in metaphase II, microtubules from opposite poles attach to the kinetochores of sister chromatids.\n(v) Correct: meiosis II starts right after cytokinesis, usually before the chromosomes have fully elongated.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-179","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":179,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Cells that enter the quiescent stage (G0), such as heart cells, become metabolically inactive.\nStatement II: In male honey bees, haploid cells divide by mitosis.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Cells in the quiescent stage (G0), such as heart cells, stay metabolically active. They simply stop proliferating unless called upon.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT gives haploid cells of male honey bees as an example of mitotic division in haploid animal cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-180","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":180,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"A diagram of a dividing animal cell shows the following: no nuclear envelope; all chromosomes lie in a single plane at the centre of the cell; each chromosome has two sister chromatids; the kinetochore of one chromatid is attached to spindle fibres from one pole, and that of its sister chromatid to fibres from the opposite pole. Which statement about this cell is correct?","image":"","options":["The homologous chromosomes are paired as bivalents and will separate next","The next event will be the splitting of centromeres and movement of chromatids to opposite poles","The cell is in late prophase, as the nucleolus and Golgi complex are still visible","The chromatids have already separated and are now called daughter chromosomes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: In mitotic metaphase there is no nuclear envelope. Chromosomes align at the equatorial plate, and the kinetochores of sister chromatids attach to spindle fibres from opposite poles.\nThe next stage is anaphase. Centromeres split and the sister chromatids (now daughter chromosomes) move to opposite poles, so (2) fits.\n(1) fails: bivalents form only in meiosis I, not mitosis.\n(3) fails: the nucleolus and Golgi complex have disappeared by late prophase, and this cell is in metaphase.\n(4) fails: chromatids are still joined at the centromere, so they are not yet daughter chromosomes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-181","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":181,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"In an animal with 2n = 40, each sperm contains 6 pg of DNA. What are the chromosome number and DNA content of a secondary spermatocyte at metaphase II?","image":"","options":["20 chromosomes, 6 pg","40 chromosomes, 12 pg","20 chromosomes, 12 pg","40 chromosomes, 24 pg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $2n = 40$. A sperm is haploid, so it has 20 chromosomes and 6 pg of DNA (= C).\nA secondary spermatocyte at metaphase II is haploid, so it has 20 chromosomes.\nEach of those chromosomes still has two sister chromatids, so its DNA = 2C = $2 \\times 6 = 12$ pg.\nOption (4), 40 chromosomes with 24 pg, describes a primary spermatocyte after S phase.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-182","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":182,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the quiescent stage (G0) are correct?\n(a) Heart cells in adult animals are an example of cells that do not appear to divide.\n(b) Cells in G0 remain metabolically active.\n(c) Cells enter G0 by exiting the G2 phase.\n(d) Cells in G0 do not proliferate unless the organism requires it.\n(e) DNA replication continues in G0 cells, but they do not undergo mitosis.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: NCERT cites heart cells of adult animals as cells that do not appear to divide.\n(b) Correct: G0 cells stay metabolically active.\n(c) Incorrect: cells enter G0 by exiting G1, not G2.\n(d) Correct: G0 cells no longer proliferate unless the organism needs it.\n(e) Incorrect: G0 cells have left the active cycle, so they are not replicating DNA.\nSo three statements, (a), (b) and (d), are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-183","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":183,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a significance of mitotic cell division?","image":"","options":["Growth of multicellular organisms","Restoration of the nucleo-cytoplasmic ratio","Replacement of worn-out cells such as those of the gut lining","Increase in genetic variability in a population from one generation to the next"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Mitosis is equational division: each daughter cell gets the same set of chromosomes as the parent.\nSo mitosis supports growth (1), restores the nucleo-cytoplasmic ratio (2) and repairs or replaces cells such as the gut lining (3).\nIt cannot create new gene combinations, so (4) is not a significance of mitosis.\nVariability comes from meiosis, through crossing over and independent assortment, together with fertilisation.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-184","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":184,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"An onion root tip cell has 16 chromosomes, and its DNA content after M phase is 2C. For this cell at mitotic metaphase, the number of chromosomes, the number of chromatids, and the DNA content respectively are:","image":"","options":["16, 32, 4C","32, 32, 4C","16, 16, 2C","8, 16, 2C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $2n = 16$ chromosomes, with 2C DNA after M phase (that is, in G1).\nS phase: DNA replicates from 2C to 4C, but the chromosome number stays 16.\nAt metaphase each chromosome has 2 sister chromatids, so there are $16 \\times 2 = 32$ chromatids.\nSo at metaphase: 16 chromosomes, 32 chromatids, 4C DNA.\nTrap: counting 32 chromosomes is wrong, because chromatids separate only at anaphase.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-CELL-CYCLE-185","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","qno":185,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Cell Cycle and Cell Division","type":"mcq","question":"An onion root tip cell has 16 chromosomes and 2C DNA content in G1. For this cell at metaphase of mitosis, which option correctly gives the number of chromosomes, the number of chromatids and the DNA content?","image":"","options":["32 chromosomes, 32 chromatids, 4C","16 chromosomes, 16 chromatids, 2C","16 chromosomes, 32 chromatids, 4C","32 chromosomes, 64 chromatids, 4C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: G1 cell with 16 chromosomes and 2C DNA.\nS phase: DNA doubles from 2C to 4C, while the chromosome number stays 16.\nAt metaphase each chromosome has two sister chromatids joined at the centromere, so there are $16 \\times 2 = 32$ chromatids.\nSo at metaphase: 16 chromosomes, 32 chromatids, 4C DNA.\nTrap: options with 32 chromosomes count chromatids as chromosomes. They become separate chromosomes only in anaphase.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 10 · Cell Cycle and Cell Division","difficulty":"hard"}]});
